WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.733
- This episode of StarTalk

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.319
is brought to you by CuriosityStream.

00:00:00.147 --> 00:00:03.007
Hey YouTubeverse, up next,

00:00:03.007 --> 00:00:05.709
Adam Savage talks about building stuff.

00:00:05.709 --> 00:00:08.292
(upbeat music)

00:00:10.337 --> 00:00:11.585
This is StarTalk.

00:00:11.585 --> 00:00:13.637
I'm your host, Neil deGrasse Tyson,

00:00:13.637 --> 00:00:15.287
your personal astrophysicist,

00:00:15.287 --> 00:00:17.597
and we're coming to you from my office

00:00:17.597 --> 00:00:18.837
at the Hayden Planetarium

00:00:18.837 --> 00:00:20.907
of the American Museum of Natural History

00:00:20.907 --> 00:00:22.527
right here in New York City.

00:00:22.527 --> 00:00:23.837
Cohost today, Chuck Nice.

00:00:23.837 --> 00:00:24.670
Chuck?

00:00:24.670 --> 00:00:26.977
- Hey Neil, always good to
be here at the cosmic crib.

00:00:26.977 --> 00:00:28.627
- [Neil] And who we making
a sandwich with here?

00:00:28.627 --> 00:00:30.367
- Ah.
- We've seen him before!

00:00:30.367 --> 00:00:31.839
- Yes we have, we did--

00:00:31.839 --> 00:00:33.942
- [Neil] The one, the
only, the inimitable.

00:00:33.942 --> 00:00:35.582
(Chuck imitates fanfare)
Adam Savage!

00:00:35.582 --> 00:00:36.717
- Yes!
- Adam, welcome back!

00:00:36.717 --> 00:00:37.834
- Thank you very much!

00:00:37.834 --> 00:00:39.207
- [Neil] It's like your
third time on StarTalk.

00:00:39.207 --> 00:00:40.614
- It's been too long since
I've been in this office.

00:00:40.614 --> 00:00:41.837
- It has been too long.

00:00:41.837 --> 00:00:44.077
We enjoyed your last
visit, and I feel like

00:00:44.077 --> 00:00:45.987
we're lifelong friends,
even though we've only had

00:00:45.987 --> 00:00:47.007
a few times together.

00:00:47.007 --> 00:00:48.337
- It feels like culturally we're--

00:00:48.337 --> 00:00:49.537
- Culturally, that's what it is.

00:00:49.537 --> 00:00:51.197
- We like bounce a lot--

00:00:51.197 --> 00:00:52.157
- Natural overlap--

00:00:52.157 --> 00:00:54.867
- The cultural Venn
diagram has quite the area

00:00:54.867 --> 00:00:56.087
where you two overlap.

00:00:56.087 --> 00:00:57.787
- And that creates the friendship

00:00:57.787 --> 00:00:59.708
even without individual time together.

00:00:59.708 --> 00:01:01.247
- I see what you're doing and I think,

00:01:01.247 --> 00:01:03.135
oh, that's awesome!
(laughter)

00:01:03.135 --> 00:01:04.347
- Cool man.

00:01:04.347 --> 00:01:05.327
- [Neil] So we've got you on.

00:01:05.327 --> 00:01:06.277
Actually you have a new book?

00:01:06.277 --> 00:01:07.607
- I do, holy cow--

00:01:07.607 --> 00:01:08.657
- I'll talk about your book.

00:01:08.657 --> 00:01:09.717
I'll do the talking about your book.

00:01:09.717 --> 00:01:10.550
- Okay, all right.
- Okay?

00:01:10.550 --> 00:01:11.817
- Okay.
- Because it looks better.

00:01:11.817 --> 00:01:12.650
Doesn't it, Chuck?

00:01:12.650 --> 00:01:14.412
- That is, that's called public relations.

00:01:14.412 --> 00:01:15.327
(laughter)

00:01:15.327 --> 00:01:17.155
When he talks about his
book, it's bragging.

00:01:17.155 --> 00:01:19.120
- It's bragging!
- When you talk about it,

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:20.420
it's public relations.
- There you go.

00:01:20.420 --> 00:01:22.660
- But enough about me, what
did you think of my book?

00:01:22.660 --> 00:01:24.467
(laughs)

00:01:24.467 --> 00:01:26.667
- So "Every Tool is a Hammer."

00:01:26.667 --> 00:01:29.717
Great title, and this, you know,

00:01:29.717 --> 00:01:31.277
my favorite picture in here

00:01:31.277 --> 00:01:33.157
is they have a lot of interesting pictures

00:01:33.157 --> 00:01:34.837
like you in your workshop.

00:01:34.837 --> 00:01:36.527
My favorite is the stuff that you dumped

00:01:36.527 --> 00:01:37.597
out of your backpack.

00:01:37.597 --> 00:01:39.110
- Oh.
- But they're neatly organized

00:01:39.110 --> 00:01:40.477
which is cool!

00:01:40.477 --> 00:01:41.847
Like seeing what's in it,

00:01:41.847 --> 00:01:44.987
and I like John Hodgman's comment.

00:01:44.987 --> 00:01:49.197
He said, "This is a map of his brain."

00:01:49.197 --> 00:01:50.620
- Nice.
- I thought that was

00:01:50.620 --> 00:01:51.677
a very good comment.

00:01:51.677 --> 00:01:54.467
- And I feel like venturing
into that unknown territory--

00:01:54.467 --> 00:01:56.957
- Yeah, for some people
you don't wanna go there.

00:01:56.957 --> 00:01:58.437
But for Adam Savage, yeah, you wanna see

00:01:58.437 --> 00:02:01.087
what's been driving him his whole life.

00:02:01.087 --> 00:02:01.957
- That is so funny.

00:02:01.957 --> 00:02:03.364
Some people, you don't wanna go there.

00:02:03.364 --> 00:02:06.847
My wife left her, she
journals, and it was out--

00:02:06.847 --> 00:02:07.797
- She journals?
- She journals.

00:02:07.797 --> 00:02:09.677
- And so the journal was on the bed--

00:02:09.677 --> 00:02:10.510
- [Neil] No, you did not!

00:02:10.510 --> 00:02:12.937
- No, I'm laying on the
bed and watching TV.

00:02:12.937 --> 00:02:14.167
The journal was sitting right there.

00:02:14.167 --> 00:02:15.394
She comes in the room and she goes,

00:02:15.394 --> 00:02:16.877
"Oh my god, I left my journal."

00:02:16.877 --> 00:02:18.504
But she goes, "You
didn't read it, did you?"

00:02:18.504 --> 00:02:21.359
And I went, "I don't wanna
know what's going on up there!"

00:02:21.359 --> 00:02:22.764
(laughter)

00:02:22.764 --> 00:02:25.644
I don't want any parts of what
is happening in your head.

00:02:25.644 --> 00:02:26.477
- Well, if your wife said,

00:02:26.477 --> 00:02:27.310
"I hope you didn't read my journal,"

00:02:27.310 --> 00:02:29.617
that does not bode well.

00:02:29.617 --> 00:02:31.747
- What movie in existence
in which someone read

00:02:31.747 --> 00:02:33.642
someone else's journal and
it all turned out great?

00:02:33.642 --> 00:02:35.033
(laughs)
- Good point!

00:02:35.033 --> 00:02:35.957
- That's so funny.

00:02:35.957 --> 00:02:37.753
- There ain't no movie about that.

00:02:37.753 --> 00:02:40.557
- My twin boys used to
like sit in their bunk beds

00:02:40.557 --> 00:02:42.544
and talk every night, and my mom was like,

00:02:42.544 --> 00:02:43.447
"What are they talking about?"

00:02:43.447 --> 00:02:45.184
I'm like, "I don't know,
that's their private moment.

00:02:45.184 --> 00:02:46.017
"I'm not gonna listen in."

00:02:46.017 --> 00:02:47.137
- Yeah, I don't wanna know,

00:02:47.137 --> 00:02:48.437
let them have their moment.

00:02:48.437 --> 00:02:50.177
- But I wanna know what's on in his head,

00:02:50.177 --> 00:02:51.827
and so does our fan base.

00:02:51.827 --> 00:02:54.582
- Now that is so true because
I have their questions.

00:02:54.582 --> 00:02:58.687
- This is a Cosmic Queries
edition of StarTalk,

00:02:58.687 --> 00:03:01.189
and we just solicited
our fan base questions,

00:03:01.189 --> 00:03:04.181
know when they'd land in
the lap of Adam Savage

00:03:04.181 --> 00:03:06.577
about just making stuff.
- Yeah.

00:03:06.577 --> 00:03:09.147
- And so, Chuck, I haven't seen them.

00:03:09.147 --> 00:03:10.317
Chuck reviewed them just this morning.

00:03:10.317 --> 00:03:12.167
- I'm actually reviewing them, and--

00:03:12.167 --> 00:03:13.697
- Adam hasn't seen them so
let's see what you've got.

00:03:13.697 --> 00:03:14.977
- Yeah, and these are cool.

00:03:14.977 --> 00:03:18.317
And as usual, we always start
with a Patreon patron because

00:03:18.317 --> 00:03:20.547
they support us--
- Because we're that low.

00:03:20.547 --> 00:03:21.967
- Well, no!
- We're that high.

00:03:21.967 --> 00:03:24.557
- We're that high, that
we have supporters.

00:03:24.557 --> 00:03:26.497
- I wanna be a member
Patreon so that I could get

00:03:26.497 --> 00:03:27.527
my questions answered by somebody.

00:03:27.527 --> 00:03:29.537
- Believe it or not, you can.

00:03:29.537 --> 00:03:31.637
You can actually donate to Patreon

00:03:31.637 --> 00:03:33.287
and then ask yourself a question.

00:03:33.287 --> 00:03:34.517
- That's how I get on that list?

00:03:34.517 --> 00:03:35.896
- Exactly.
- That's what I will do.

00:03:35.896 --> 00:03:38.067
I will ask myself. (laughs)

00:03:38.067 --> 00:03:38.900
- That would be funny.

00:03:38.900 --> 00:03:39.733
- All right, I'll do that.

00:03:39.733 --> 00:03:40.566
- All right, here we go.

00:03:40.566 --> 00:03:42.584
This is Bike and Birds says,

00:03:42.584 --> 00:03:45.154
"Hey, Adam, we've all heard of examples

00:03:45.154 --> 00:03:49.264
"of items or procedures
getting discovered by accident.

00:03:49.264 --> 00:03:52.864
"Microwaves, penicillin,
even chocolate chip cookies.

00:03:52.864 --> 00:03:55.094
"Through all of your
different experiments,

00:03:55.094 --> 00:03:58.784
"was there an outcome or
product that was produced

00:03:58.784 --> 00:04:02.454
"that was applied to another test

00:04:02.454 --> 00:04:06.896
"or maybe even applications
outside of the show?"

00:04:06.896 --> 00:04:07.857
And this is--

00:04:07.857 --> 00:04:10.273
- Is that kind of like, did he
invent anything by accident?

00:04:10.273 --> 00:04:11.897
- [Chuck] Did you ever
invent anything by accident?

00:04:11.897 --> 00:04:14.123
- It was a long way, it was
a long walk around the block.

00:04:14.123 --> 00:04:16.017
(overlapping dialogue)
- Off the off ramp,

00:04:16.017 --> 00:04:18.697
he took the cloverleaf to get back on.

00:04:18.697 --> 00:04:20.017
- I have a comment and a question

00:04:20.017 --> 00:04:22.057
but an autobiographical story first.

00:04:22.057 --> 00:04:24.843
(chuckles)
Actually--

00:04:24.843 --> 00:04:26.307
- By the way, there's a
word for that in science.

00:04:26.307 --> 00:04:27.820
It's serendipity.
- Okay.

00:04:27.820 --> 00:04:29.250
- Yes.
- A great word.

00:04:30.157 --> 00:04:32.112
There was a moment.

00:04:32.112 --> 00:04:34.627
I can't think of anything
we invented by accident,

00:04:34.627 --> 00:04:35.607
but I do remember we were on--

00:04:35.607 --> 00:04:37.807
- Up on Discovery.
- We were on Hawaii

00:04:37.807 --> 00:04:40.437
on MythBusters shooting
"Duct Tape Island,"

00:04:40.437 --> 00:04:43.747
and this was the conceit
Jamie and I get stranded on--

00:04:43.747 --> 00:04:46.767
- Jaimie your co-host from MythBusters.

00:04:46.767 --> 00:04:49.197
- So Jamie Hyneman and I get
stranded on a deserted island

00:04:49.197 --> 00:04:50.747
and all we have is a palette

00:04:50.747 --> 00:04:52.727
of several hundred rolls of duct tape.

00:04:52.727 --> 00:04:53.670
What do we do?

00:04:54.543 --> 00:04:55.702
(laughter)

00:04:55.702 --> 00:04:56.837
- Wait, wait, wait.

00:04:56.837 --> 00:04:59.706
So is MacGyver tuning in to this episode?

00:04:59.706 --> 00:05:01.697
More things to do with duct tape.

00:05:01.697 --> 00:05:03.817
- Exactly, so we made shelter,

00:05:03.817 --> 00:05:06.467
we actually made traps
and caught chickens.

00:05:06.467 --> 00:05:10.277
We ended up making a 21-foot
long outrigger canoe,

00:05:10.277 --> 00:05:12.767
with which we were able
to get past the breakwater

00:05:12.767 --> 00:05:14.767
of the North Shore of Oahu.

00:05:14.767 --> 00:05:16.787
But there was a moment
when Jamie was asked

00:05:16.787 --> 00:05:21.247
to make a still for
distilling clean water.

00:05:21.247 --> 00:05:23.717
Sorry, fresh water from the salt water.

00:05:23.717 --> 00:05:26.497
And so he was digging into
the beach to make a hole,

00:05:26.497 --> 00:05:28.647
and the procedure is
you dig into the beach,

00:05:28.647 --> 00:05:29.480
you make a hole.

00:05:29.480 --> 00:05:30.677
It's gonna be kind of a damp hole

00:05:30.677 --> 00:05:33.057
because it's on the
beach, and you let sit in

00:05:33.057 --> 00:05:34.977
some plastic with a rock in the middle.

00:05:34.977 --> 00:05:36.897
And as water condenses from,

00:05:36.897 --> 00:05:39.187
the salt water collects on the inside

00:05:39.187 --> 00:05:41.757
of the membrane you've put,
you can catch it in a cup

00:05:41.757 --> 00:05:43.707
and drink fresh water.

00:05:43.707 --> 00:05:46.057
However, Jamie noticed that when he dug in

00:05:46.057 --> 00:05:48.557
and tasted just the water
that was being filtered

00:05:48.557 --> 00:05:52.027
through the sand, that it was
much less brackish and salty.

00:05:52.027 --> 00:05:54.697
And so he got really
excited about the idea

00:05:54.697 --> 00:05:58.757
that the sand of the beach
itself was filtering the water.

00:05:58.757 --> 00:05:59.590
- Interesting.

00:05:59.590 --> 00:06:02.277
- And we explained that
this was a whole episode

00:06:02.277 --> 00:06:03.177
about duct tape.

00:06:03.177 --> 00:06:04.837
And while this was really interesting,

00:06:04.837 --> 00:06:07.467
it really didn't fit within our narrative.

00:06:07.467 --> 00:06:09.837
But he kept insisting and
so we ended up shooting

00:06:09.837 --> 00:06:11.127
this whole sequence with Jamie,

00:06:11.127 --> 00:06:13.067
which I think we put on the web

00:06:13.067 --> 00:06:14.817
because that was the most interesting part

00:06:14.817 --> 00:06:16.957
of that day for him, was
the idea that the sand

00:06:16.957 --> 00:06:18.257
filtered the salt out.

00:06:18.257 --> 00:06:20.524
But this was a classic
thing of Jamie going,

00:06:20.524 --> 00:06:21.847
"This is the thing I'm interested in,"

00:06:21.847 --> 00:06:22.954
and we're saying, "No, that's not what

00:06:22.954 --> 00:06:23.847
"the episode is about."

00:06:23.847 --> 00:06:25.574
He's like, "I don't
care what you're saying,

00:06:25.574 --> 00:06:27.427
"I just wanna talk about this thing."

00:06:27.427 --> 00:06:29.501
- And now I love all my water gritty.

00:06:29.501 --> 00:06:31.214
(laughs)
- Yes, just a little bit--

00:06:31.214 --> 00:06:35.847
- I need just a little bit
of grit in all of my water.

00:06:35.847 --> 00:06:37.687
- I'll drink it out of my boot, thank you.

00:06:37.687 --> 00:06:38.520
- Cool.
- All right, cool.

00:06:38.520 --> 00:06:39.353
Next question.
- That's a great story.

00:06:39.353 --> 00:06:40.430
Great story, great story.

00:06:40.430 --> 00:06:43.494
This is John Cole from Facebook who says,

00:06:43.494 --> 00:06:47.274
"Between StarTalk and the
Tested YouTube channel,

00:06:47.274 --> 00:06:49.234
"I am in heaven!

00:06:49.234 --> 00:06:50.584
"Hello, Adam.

00:06:50.584 --> 00:06:54.964
"How do you get over
the maker's equivalent

00:06:54.964 --> 00:06:56.497
"of writer's block?"

00:06:56.497 --> 00:06:59.306
- Oh, good one!
- What a great question!

00:06:59.306 --> 00:07:00.139
- Good one.

00:07:00.139 --> 00:07:02.117
- John Cole, you are thinking, my friend.

00:07:02.117 --> 00:07:03.924
- [Neil] So do you ever just
walk in your garage and say,

00:07:03.924 --> 00:07:05.687
"I don't know what I'm
gonna put together today."

00:07:05.687 --> 00:07:06.897
- Totally.

00:07:06.897 --> 00:07:09.627
But more than that, I also
hit moments where I spend

00:07:09.627 --> 00:07:11.567
a whole day assembling
something and realized

00:07:11.567 --> 00:07:14.807
that I have assembled
it chirally backwards

00:07:14.807 --> 00:07:16.007
and that I have to take the whole--

00:07:16.007 --> 00:07:17.488
(overlapping dialogue)
- Explain what chirally means.

00:07:17.488 --> 00:07:18.867
- Well, chirally is left and right.

00:07:18.867 --> 00:07:20.144
Your hands are chiral because--

00:07:20.144 --> 00:07:21.947
- Let Chuck explain.
- I'm so sorry.

00:07:21.947 --> 00:07:22.959
Chuck, what is chiral?

00:07:22.959 --> 00:07:26.697
- It's the mirror image
without the mirror.

00:07:26.697 --> 00:07:27.657
- Nice!

00:07:27.657 --> 00:07:29.230
- Ooh, that was much more concise.

00:07:29.230 --> 00:07:31.552
- That was way better than
where you were going with that.

00:07:31.552 --> 00:07:32.385
- Yeah, it was.

00:07:32.385 --> 00:07:33.587
I was being way too wordy.

00:07:33.587 --> 00:07:36.267
- You can make something that
is the mirror image of itself

00:07:36.267 --> 00:07:37.507
and then you hold them together

00:07:37.507 --> 00:07:38.897
and they're mirror images.
- Exactly.

00:07:38.897 --> 00:07:42.537
- And our amino acids have one chirality,

00:07:42.537 --> 00:07:45.517
and they all have the same
chirality for all life on Earth.

00:07:45.517 --> 00:07:49.497
And when we found amino
acids in asteroids,

00:07:49.497 --> 00:07:52.877
we found that they were
50/50 of both chiralities.

00:07:52.877 --> 00:07:55.317
So we knew that it didn't care

00:07:55.317 --> 00:07:56.967
and there wasn't one life form,

00:07:56.967 --> 00:07:59.617
because amino acids aren't alive yet,

00:07:59.617 --> 00:08:00.787
that over ran the other.

00:08:00.787 --> 00:08:03.837
So our chirality is the one
chirality in the whole world.

00:08:03.837 --> 00:08:05.977
And there's suspicions that
if there was another life form

00:08:05.977 --> 00:08:08.747
with the other chirality,
of our molecules,

00:08:08.747 --> 00:08:11.297
that you wouldn't be
able to metabolize them

00:08:11.297 --> 00:08:14.665
if you ate them because the
molecules wouldn't fit together.

00:08:14.665 --> 00:08:15.498
(overlapping dialogue)

00:08:15.498 --> 00:08:16.747
Might not even be able to taste it.

00:08:16.747 --> 00:08:17.634
- Oh that's cool.

00:08:17.634 --> 00:08:20.607
- And they would probably be like a guy,

00:08:20.607 --> 00:08:22.858
one half of his face will be black

00:08:22.858 --> 00:08:25.687
and the other half of
his face would be white.

00:08:25.687 --> 00:08:27.177
- It's really angry and sweet.

00:08:27.177 --> 00:08:28.320
- Right, exactly.

00:08:30.437 --> 00:08:31.887
- So from a standpoint of making,

00:08:31.887 --> 00:08:33.827
whenever you have two
parts that are chiral,

00:08:33.827 --> 00:08:36.947
it can be a devilishly
difficult assembly problem

00:08:36.947 --> 00:08:39.317
because everything looks very similar

00:08:39.317 --> 00:08:41.907
but the order of
operations is very precise.

00:08:41.907 --> 00:08:42.854
- Tetris is kind of like that,

00:08:42.854 --> 00:08:45.097
two of the pieces are
mirror opposite each other.

00:08:45.097 --> 00:08:45.930
- Right.
- Right.

00:08:45.930 --> 00:08:47.897
So regularly in making,
you hit a spot where you--

00:08:47.897 --> 00:08:50.741
- [Neil] I'm sorry, 3D
Tetris I was thinking, sorry.

00:08:50.741 --> 00:08:51.863
(chuckles)

00:08:51.863 --> 00:08:53.507
- So you hit a spot where
you've screwed something up

00:08:53.507 --> 00:08:56.127
and you feel, I feel
really dejected about it

00:08:56.127 --> 00:08:57.637
and I don't feel like moving forward

00:08:57.637 --> 00:09:00.207
or I don't feel like
undoing the five hours

00:09:00.207 --> 00:09:02.200
of work I've just done and I'm angry,

00:09:03.317 --> 00:09:05.510
I'm pissed off, I feel sad about it.

00:09:06.647 --> 00:09:08.047
- That's funny because I feel the same way

00:09:08.047 --> 00:09:10.362
whenever I put anything
together from IKEA.

00:09:10.362 --> 00:09:12.445
(laughs)

00:09:16.007 --> 00:09:17.297
- Chuck, the table is upside down.

00:09:17.297 --> 00:09:19.400
- Really?
- Yes, totally!

00:09:19.400 --> 00:09:23.327
IKEA is absolutely ripe for
that kind of screwing up.

00:09:23.327 --> 00:09:24.647
- But go ahead.

00:09:24.647 --> 00:09:27.277
- And how does everything
fit in a flat box anyway?

00:09:27.277 --> 00:09:28.537
Isn't there something
that should not be able

00:09:28.537 --> 00:09:29.630
to fit in a flat box?

00:09:31.357 --> 00:09:32.807
- The IKEA car would go boop.

00:09:33.767 --> 00:09:34.837
- All right, sorry.
- Go ahead.

00:09:34.837 --> 00:09:39.837
- There's a line from a Mary
Karr who wrote "Liars' Club",

00:09:40.497 --> 00:09:44.117
wrote about what she
does with writer's block.

00:09:44.117 --> 00:09:45.934
And she says, "If I can't
think about what to write,

00:09:45.934 --> 00:09:47.814
"I sit at my desk and I copy writers

00:09:47.814 --> 00:09:50.834
"I love in longhand because
my fidelity at the desk

00:09:50.834 --> 00:09:54.217
"is to be writing, whether
it's my own writing or not."

00:09:54.217 --> 00:09:55.347
- What an exercise!

00:09:55.347 --> 00:09:57.407
- It's a beautiful
exercise and I take that

00:09:57.407 --> 00:09:58.240
to heart in the shop.

00:09:58.240 --> 00:09:59.417
If I can't think of what to make,

00:09:59.417 --> 00:10:01.267
I organize something in my shop.

00:10:01.267 --> 00:10:03.637
A drawer, a shelf, a bin.

00:10:03.637 --> 00:10:06.337
I take something and I adjust it because--

00:10:06.337 --> 00:10:07.470
- So you do shop time no matter what.

00:10:07.470 --> 00:10:10.067
- I do shop time and I
do so much more shop time

00:10:10.067 --> 00:10:11.997
than I ever did before because I realized

00:10:11.997 --> 00:10:14.037
it's a deep part of that whole process,

00:10:14.037 --> 00:10:16.737
of prepping the shop and
prepping myself for the work.

00:10:16.737 --> 00:10:18.177
- I do a little bit something like that.

00:10:18.177 --> 00:10:21.387
I have a book called "The
Greatest Wits of All Time"

00:10:21.387 --> 00:10:24.197
and they're phrases out of
letters and correspondence

00:10:24.197 --> 00:10:25.837
from people that have extraordinary wit.

00:10:25.837 --> 00:10:26.670
And I just read it.

00:10:26.670 --> 00:10:29.507
I say, wow, that was an
awesome juxtaposition

00:10:29.507 --> 00:10:32.087
of words and phrases or rhythm,

00:10:32.087 --> 00:10:35.888
and that just sort of
rebaptizes me into a mood

00:10:35.888 --> 00:10:38.017
that I then say, okay, I'm ready--

00:10:38.017 --> 00:10:39.007
- [Adam] That feels fertile.

00:10:39.007 --> 00:10:40.747
- There you go, I like that.

00:10:40.747 --> 00:10:43.247
- So do you ever suffer
writer's block, Neil?

00:10:43.247 --> 00:10:44.537
- No, no, I go through this exercise.

00:10:44.537 --> 00:10:47.777
I have a lot of books written by writers

00:10:47.777 --> 00:10:49.527
who like writing about writing,

00:10:49.527 --> 00:10:51.887
also about whatever else they wrote about.

00:10:51.887 --> 00:10:53.198
Did that sentence make any--

00:10:53.198 --> 00:10:54.798
(laughs)
- It sounds very meta.

00:10:55.801 --> 00:10:57.247
- There's a lot of inception today.

00:10:57.247 --> 00:10:58.449
- I would never wanna write that sentence.

00:10:58.449 --> 00:10:59.893
That's only a speakable sentence.

00:10:59.893 --> 00:11:01.777
And there's one sentence that,

00:11:01.777 --> 00:11:04.577
I think I uttered this
in our last recording,

00:11:04.577 --> 00:11:07.127
a sentence from "The Great Gatsby."

00:11:07.127 --> 00:11:10.247
This is a sentence where I
knew I still have not become

00:11:10.247 --> 00:11:12.657
a great writer, and maybe never will.

00:11:12.657 --> 00:11:14.117
- [Chuck] Oh wow, okay.

00:11:14.117 --> 00:11:15.840
I have to hear this now.

00:11:16.774 --> 00:11:21.534
- "In his blue gardens men and girls

00:11:21.534 --> 00:11:24.567
"came and went like moths

00:11:26.094 --> 00:11:30.734
"amid the whispers, the
champagne and the stars."

00:11:32.227 --> 00:11:33.060
- That's a great--

00:11:33.060 --> 00:11:37.937
- I read that, that's why I
am not a novelist and he is.

00:11:39.387 --> 00:11:41.707
- You reminded me of one
my favorite passages from--

00:11:41.707 --> 00:11:45.277
- Obviously this was from the
Great Gatsby's, his party.

00:11:45.277 --> 00:11:46.110
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

00:11:46.110 --> 00:11:48.064
- You reminded me of a line from Chandler.

00:11:48.064 --> 00:11:50.367
- That's like a perfect simile.

00:11:50.367 --> 00:11:52.439
- Plus, he mentioned--
- Came and went as moths.

00:11:52.439 --> 00:11:53.617
- Yes, as moths.
- That's beautiful.

00:11:53.617 --> 00:11:55.767
It's very elegant.
- Amid the whispers of

00:11:55.767 --> 00:11:58.697
the champagne and the stars.
- Incredible.

00:11:58.697 --> 00:12:00.797
- You know, I didn't come
here to feel inadequate.

00:12:00.797 --> 00:12:02.850
(laughs)
- And yet.

00:12:02.850 --> 00:12:05.004
- And yet here we are.

00:12:07.553 --> 00:12:08.714
- So what were you saying, Adam?

00:12:08.714 --> 00:12:10.777
- I'm saying it reminds me
of this line from Chandler

00:12:10.777 --> 00:12:12.897
where his hero, Marlowe, is
sitting in a waiting room

00:12:12.897 --> 00:12:15.764
for too long and he describes
the time passing as,

00:12:15.764 --> 00:12:20.467
"the minutes tiptoed by
their fingers to their lips."

00:12:20.467 --> 00:12:22.174
- Lovely.
- Ooh yeah.

00:12:23.087 --> 00:12:24.807
- Okay, so I have one.

00:12:24.807 --> 00:12:26.600
It's a quote from my father.

00:12:27.654 --> 00:12:30.344
"Seriously, what the hell
are you still doing here?"

00:12:30.344 --> 00:12:33.247
(laughter)

00:12:33.247 --> 00:12:35.467
- You haven't moved out
of the basement yet?

00:12:35.467 --> 00:12:37.117
- That's a talk every father has.

00:12:37.957 --> 00:12:38.790
- All right.

00:12:38.790 --> 00:12:40.127
- I'll just remind people,

00:12:40.127 --> 00:12:42.287
we got Adam here because
he's got a new book.

00:12:42.287 --> 00:12:44.107
We would have him anyway
even without the book,

00:12:44.107 --> 00:12:45.377
let's make that clear.
- I appreciate that.

00:12:45.377 --> 00:12:46.967
- Okay, but he was made available to us

00:12:46.967 --> 00:12:48.500
by Simon &amp; Schuster, okay?

00:12:50.307 --> 00:12:51.638
For free, right?

00:12:51.638 --> 00:12:53.457
He was like in New York doing media,

00:12:53.457 --> 00:12:54.677
and I guess we count as media.

00:12:54.677 --> 00:12:55.907
- You do!
- We do, yeah.

00:12:55.907 --> 00:12:57.430
Nice.
- I can handle that.

00:12:58.384 --> 00:13:01.867
- "Every Tool is a Hammer:
Life is What You Make It."

00:13:01.867 --> 00:13:03.038
I see what he did there.

00:13:03.038 --> 00:13:04.307
- Yeah, that's very cool.
- Right, right.

00:13:04.307 --> 00:13:05.917
So, Adam--
- "Every Tool is a Hammer"

00:13:05.917 --> 00:13:07.447
actually does sound like something

00:13:07.447 --> 00:13:08.647
Donald Trump would say though.

00:13:08.647 --> 00:13:09.819
I'm just saying.

00:13:09.819 --> 00:13:10.745
(laughter)

00:13:10.745 --> 00:13:12.794
- No, the phrase of mine he's grabbed is,

00:13:12.794 --> 00:13:15.436
"I reject your reality
and substitute my own."

00:13:15.436 --> 00:13:17.207
(laughter)

00:13:17.207 --> 00:13:18.040
- Very nice.

00:13:19.601 --> 00:13:21.107
- Is that a quote from you?
- That is.

00:13:21.107 --> 00:13:23.037
- He actually quoted that?

00:13:23.037 --> 00:13:24.843
- No, he hasn't quoted that but--

00:13:24.843 --> 00:13:29.497
(overlapping dialogue)
- I love it.

00:13:29.497 --> 00:13:33.217
All right, let's take Mitch
Morris who comes to us--

00:13:33.217 --> 00:13:34.747
- You're doing well with the names today.

00:13:34.747 --> 00:13:36.757
- That's because they're regular names!

00:13:36.757 --> 00:13:37.784
- Yeah, regular.

00:13:37.784 --> 00:13:39.246
How racist can you get?

00:13:39.246 --> 00:13:41.307
- I can get a lot more racist.

00:13:41.307 --> 00:13:44.819
I'm just letting you
know, since we're asking.

00:13:44.819 --> 00:13:45.652
(laughter)

00:13:45.652 --> 00:13:46.684
- All right, go on.

00:13:47.774 --> 00:13:52.437
- But you are right, it's
extremely, what do you call it,

00:13:52.437 --> 00:13:54.307
presumptive of me.
- Yes, yes.

00:13:54.307 --> 00:13:55.140
- All right, here we go.

00:13:55.140 --> 00:13:57.351
Mitch Morris from Instagram.

00:13:57.351 --> 00:13:59.601
(laughter)

00:14:01.774 --> 00:14:05.047
Mitch Morris from Instagram
wants to know this.

00:14:05.047 --> 00:14:07.987
What is the most important
tool ever invented?

00:14:07.987 --> 00:14:09.337
That's for the both of you.

00:14:09.337 --> 00:14:10.977
What an interesting little question.

00:14:10.977 --> 00:14:13.620
Very simple little question
but very interesting.

00:14:14.617 --> 00:14:15.857
I'll answer first because I want him

00:14:15.857 --> 00:14:18.007
to bring closure to the question.

00:14:18.007 --> 00:14:20.267
So when I was younger, I said, wow,

00:14:20.267 --> 00:14:23.400
this crescent wrench is
really useful, right?

00:14:23.400 --> 00:14:24.875
Because you can adjust it.

00:14:24.875 --> 00:14:27.247
And then I discovered the--

00:14:27.247 --> 00:14:30.127
- Miter saw?
- No, no, no, the--

00:14:30.127 --> 00:14:31.401
- Vise grip.
- The vise grip!

00:14:31.401 --> 00:14:32.357
- Oh!
- Oh.

00:14:32.357 --> 00:14:34.644
- And I said, oh, I've died
and gone to heaven, vise grip.

00:14:34.644 --> 00:14:36.524
And then, and then--

00:14:38.047 --> 00:14:39.247
- [Chuck] You got better than vise grip?

00:14:39.247 --> 00:14:40.497
- Yes!
- Okay.

00:14:40.497 --> 00:14:42.537
- The most important tool is money

00:14:42.537 --> 00:14:44.619
to pay somebody else to fix it!

00:14:44.619 --> 00:14:47.109
(laughter)

00:14:47.109 --> 00:14:49.457
That became the most important tool.

00:14:49.457 --> 00:14:50.963
I ain't fixing this plumbing.

00:14:50.963 --> 00:14:52.587
I'm gonna go hire a plumber.

00:14:52.587 --> 00:14:53.957
That's gonna be my tool kit for--

00:14:53.957 --> 00:14:55.577
- Wow, capital!
(laughter)

00:14:55.577 --> 00:14:57.580
You may have just answered the question.

00:14:58.957 --> 00:15:01.097
- I have to say in the
circles I travel in,

00:15:01.097 --> 00:15:03.990
a crescent wrench is known
as a nut corner rounder,

00:15:05.017 --> 00:15:07.827
and the vise grips are
known as the professional

00:15:07.827 --> 00:15:09.597
nut corner rounder.

00:15:09.597 --> 00:15:11.737
They can mess up, don't get me wrong,

00:15:11.737 --> 00:15:13.022
I love both of those tools.

00:15:13.022 --> 00:15:13.977
- Corner rounder (mumbles) your hex

00:15:13.977 --> 00:15:15.397
and removing the edges to it--

00:15:15.397 --> 00:15:17.312
- Yeah, until they can no longer be--

00:15:17.312 --> 00:15:18.672
- Right, a nut corner rounder.

00:15:18.672 --> 00:15:20.237
- A nut corner rounder.
- You're right.

00:15:20.237 --> 00:15:22.627
- I have to go to the simplest,

00:15:22.627 --> 00:15:24.997
I mean one of the simple
machines, the lever.

00:15:24.997 --> 00:15:27.387
I feel like the ability
to move heavy objects

00:15:27.387 --> 00:15:29.597
with a mechanical advantage,

00:15:29.597 --> 00:15:34.597
and the lever exists before the
wheel or any rollers to me--

00:15:34.857 --> 00:15:37.134
- It's Archimedes who said...

00:15:37.134 --> 00:15:38.894
- "Give me a lever and a place to stand

00:15:38.894 --> 00:15:39.947
"and I can move the world."

00:15:39.947 --> 00:15:40.780
- I don't think he said lever.

00:15:40.780 --> 00:15:42.417
He just said, "Give me a place to stand."

00:15:42.417 --> 00:15:43.250
- Give me a place,

00:15:43.250 --> 00:15:44.937
or I think he was describing the--

00:15:44.937 --> 00:15:49.077
- Yes of course, but I'm
saying that was implicit.

00:15:49.077 --> 00:15:50.409
- Okay!
- Give me a lever and a place.

00:15:50.409 --> 00:15:52.527
Just give me a place to stand

00:15:52.527 --> 00:15:54.887
and I can move the world.

00:15:54.887 --> 00:15:56.317
Beautiful.
- There you go.

00:15:56.317 --> 00:15:58.757
- Yeah, yeah, next question.

00:15:58.757 --> 00:15:59.837
Right, good stuff so far.

00:15:59.837 --> 00:16:01.154
- Okay, lever, I'll give you a lever.

00:16:01.154 --> 00:16:02.547
We do the lever.

00:16:02.547 --> 00:16:03.947
Next question.

00:16:03.947 --> 00:16:04.780
- Here's the next question

00:16:04.780 --> 00:16:05.740
from (mumbles) Wumoshop.

00:16:08.390 --> 00:16:09.557
Wumoshop, yes.

00:16:11.235 --> 00:16:14.159
(laughter)
- Sounds problematic.

00:16:14.159 --> 00:16:17.247
- Do you have a brain injury
that's presenting with words?

00:16:17.247 --> 00:16:18.464
- I think I just had a stroke.

00:16:18.464 --> 00:16:19.627
(overlapping dialogue)

00:16:19.627 --> 00:16:21.024
- Wumoshop.

00:16:21.024 --> 00:16:21.924
"Hi Neil, I'm Adam.

00:16:21.924 --> 00:16:23.964
"You guys are both my heroes.

00:16:23.964 --> 00:16:27.124
"Which would you say would
be the more important

00:16:27.124 --> 00:16:29.064
"for the future of invention?

00:16:29.064 --> 00:16:31.234
"Good old fashioned mechanical know how

00:16:31.234 --> 00:16:35.364
"or advancements in material science,

00:16:35.364 --> 00:16:37.834
"i.e., inventing a new
type of suspension bridge

00:16:37.834 --> 00:16:39.054
"that is stronger and lighter

00:16:39.054 --> 00:16:42.935
"versus discovering a stronger
and lighter titanium alloy?"

00:16:42.935 --> 00:16:44.897
- Ooh, okay, we don't
have time to answer that.

00:16:44.897 --> 00:16:46.480
- Oh! (laughter)

00:16:46.480 --> 00:16:47.407
- Where do you go, Wumoshop?

00:16:47.407 --> 00:16:48.240
