00:18:24.220 --> 00:18:25.707
And before we went to
break, we had a question

00:18:25.707 --> 00:18:28.504
from Wumoshop who says,

00:18:28.504 --> 00:18:29.614
"Hi Neil, I'm Adam.

00:18:29.614 --> 00:18:31.114
"You guys are both my heroes.

00:18:31.114 --> 00:18:32.734
"Which would you say would
be the more important

00:18:32.734 --> 00:18:33.864
"for the future of invention?

00:18:33.864 --> 00:18:35.494
"Good old fashioned mechanical know how

00:18:35.494 --> 00:18:37.527
"or advancements in material science."

00:18:37.527 --> 00:18:38.847
- [Neil] And go.

00:18:38.847 --> 00:18:40.467
- Good old fashioned mechanical know how.

00:18:40.467 --> 00:18:42.887
We are always gonna be
advancing in material science,

00:18:42.887 --> 00:18:44.280
and there will sometimes be things

00:18:44.280 --> 00:18:45.877
that we cannot replicate

00:18:45.877 --> 00:18:49.727
because we lose the technology
or we forget a process.

00:18:49.727 --> 00:18:51.487
But as long as we have a deep foundation

00:18:51.487 --> 00:18:53.879
in mechanical knowledge, in making things

00:18:53.879 --> 00:18:55.437
and the roots and the physics

00:18:55.437 --> 00:18:56.647
of putting stuff together,

00:18:56.647 --> 00:18:58.577
we can utilize those advances

00:18:58.577 --> 00:19:00.247
and we can adjust to the changes

00:19:00.247 --> 00:19:02.600
in the available materials as they come.

00:19:04.037 --> 00:19:05.657
- Wow, great answer.

00:19:05.657 --> 00:19:06.527
- I only kind of agree with that.

00:19:06.527 --> 00:19:09.267
- Uh-oh.
- I'm agreeing 50%.

00:19:09.267 --> 00:19:11.468
- Oh, okay.

00:19:11.468 --> 00:19:13.977
- I'm into material science.

00:19:13.977 --> 00:19:16.027
It is one of the most underappreciated

00:19:16.027 --> 00:19:19.570
unheralded branches of
science in this world.

00:19:20.907 --> 00:19:22.680
If you go back 50 years,

00:19:23.967 --> 00:19:28.867
hardly any, now I'm not
bragging about this advance,

00:19:28.867 --> 00:19:31.177
I'm just citing it as a
difference in our lives.

00:19:31.177 --> 00:19:34.687
You go back 50 years, hardly
anything was made of plastic.

00:19:34.687 --> 00:19:35.520
- [Chuck] Correct.

00:19:35.520 --> 00:19:38.997
- And almost everything
is made of plastic today.

00:19:38.997 --> 00:19:40.147
- And it's working out great.

00:19:40.147 --> 00:19:41.190
- For many things,

00:19:41.190 --> 00:19:43.837
as long as you don't have
to throw it away, okay?

00:19:43.837 --> 00:19:44.670
It's working out great.

00:19:44.670 --> 00:19:46.887
The plastic is stronger,
it's more reliable

00:19:46.887 --> 00:19:49.797
than other materials and you can mold it.

00:19:49.797 --> 00:19:54.184
And just think of the
things that having simple

00:19:54.184 --> 00:19:59.184
"standard mechanical
knowledge" would, so what?

00:19:59.194 --> 00:20:01.489
Look what this material can do.

00:20:01.489 --> 00:20:04.097
There are materials now that have memory

00:20:04.097 --> 00:20:05.967
of a shape they once had

00:20:05.967 --> 00:20:06.827
so you can deform it--

00:20:06.827 --> 00:20:08.567
- I sleep on it.
- Then you'd like wet it

00:20:08.567 --> 00:20:11.056
and then it goes back
to the previous shape.

00:20:11.056 --> 00:20:15.297
Nothing in your lab,
nothing in your garage

00:20:15.297 --> 00:20:16.130
is ready for that.

00:20:16.130 --> 00:20:19.227
- But within the one is
better than the other,

00:20:19.227 --> 00:20:24.047
mechanical knowledge can push
humanity forward on its own,

00:20:24.047 --> 00:20:26.327
whereas material science
can't necessarily.

00:20:26.327 --> 00:20:27.177
- With the good materials,

00:20:27.177 --> 00:20:29.401
you don't need the mechanical knowledge.

00:20:29.401 --> 00:20:31.087
It's built into the material itself.

00:20:31.087 --> 00:20:33.059
- I totally disagree with that.

00:20:33.059 --> 00:20:34.156
- Smackdown!

00:20:34.156 --> 00:20:35.627
(laughter)

00:20:35.627 --> 00:20:37.518
Meet me in the Octagon.

00:20:37.518 --> 00:20:38.351
Smackdown.

00:20:39.617 --> 00:20:42.897
- So as an aside, I
just finished a new show

00:20:42.897 --> 00:20:45.077
for the Science Channel
called Savage Builds

00:20:45.077 --> 00:20:47.157
in which I build absurd things every week.

00:20:47.157 --> 00:20:51.277
In the first episode, we
made a suit of Iron Man armor

00:20:51.277 --> 00:20:53.657
in 3D printed titanium.

00:20:53.657 --> 00:20:55.097
And speaking of material science,

00:20:55.097 --> 00:20:57.797
I discovered that when
you 3D print Titanium,

00:20:57.797 --> 00:21:00.177
you can attenuate it's grain structure

00:21:00.177 --> 00:21:03.869
so that it is far stronger
than normal titanium

00:21:03.869 --> 00:21:06.577
because you can give it these super tight

00:21:06.577 --> 00:21:07.697
little crystallin granules.

00:21:07.697 --> 00:21:08.890
- Get closer.
- Yeah.

00:21:08.890 --> 00:21:10.055
- Make it tighter.

00:21:10.055 --> 00:21:10.888
- Wait, wait, wait,

00:21:10.888 --> 00:21:13.594
except that makes it
denser it seems to me.

00:21:13.594 --> 00:21:15.567
- It didn't in this case.

00:21:15.567 --> 00:21:17.600
No, it's still 45% the
weight of steel and--

00:21:17.600 --> 00:21:18.467
- Interesting, okay, okay--

00:21:18.467 --> 00:21:19.300
- As stronger and stronger.

00:21:19.300 --> 00:21:20.267
- If you made it denser, you would lose

00:21:20.267 --> 00:21:21.667
some of the value of it being light?

00:21:21.667 --> 00:21:22.500
- Yeah, no.

00:21:22.500 --> 00:21:23.333
This stuff was amazing.

00:21:23.333 --> 00:21:25.927
We had potato chip-thin pieces.

00:21:25.927 --> 00:21:27.630
They were bullet proof.
- What?

00:21:28.659 --> 00:21:30.021
- Bullets slid right off of this.

00:21:30.021 --> 00:21:30.947
- Now wait a minute,

00:21:30.947 --> 00:21:34.254
is this something that
was already discovered

00:21:34.254 --> 00:21:35.256
or is it just something

00:21:35.256 --> 00:21:36.089
that you discovered.
- Oh yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:21:36.089 --> 00:21:37.177
No, GE is using 3D printed titanium

00:21:37.177 --> 00:21:39.022
in their jet engines right now.

00:21:39.022 --> 00:21:40.424
- Okay.
- Absolutely.

00:21:40.424 --> 00:21:41.826
- Yeah, what if he
accidentally discovered--

00:21:41.826 --> 00:21:43.854
(overlapping dialogue)

00:21:43.854 --> 00:21:45.837
- It's a property that
you found for yourself.

00:21:45.837 --> 00:21:47.907
- I was working with--
(overlapping dialogue)

00:21:47.907 --> 00:21:49.497
There's an amazing engineering school

00:21:49.497 --> 00:21:51.587
called the Colorado School of Minds.

00:21:51.587 --> 00:21:52.477
- Yes, everyone knows that, yes.

00:21:52.477 --> 00:21:53.797
- Those guys are incredible,

00:21:53.797 --> 00:21:55.737
and I worked directly with them

00:21:55.737 --> 00:21:57.877
in their Additive
Manufacturing Department.

00:21:57.877 --> 00:21:59.457
- Okay, so he just figured out

00:21:59.457 --> 00:22:02.167
a brilliant new material

00:22:02.167 --> 00:22:04.232
to make armor for knights.

00:22:04.232 --> 00:22:06.482
(laughter)

00:22:07.520 --> 00:22:08.787
600 years late.

00:22:08.787 --> 00:22:09.620
- Exactly, man.

00:22:11.397 --> 00:22:12.757
- That's great.

00:22:12.757 --> 00:22:15.869
- Yeah, but I can't wait
to see what new material

00:22:15.869 --> 00:22:18.487
is coming down the pipe and how that might

00:22:18.487 --> 00:22:19.517
transform how we live.

00:22:19.517 --> 00:22:20.567
I think about that all the time.

00:22:20.567 --> 00:22:21.839
- Okay, all right.

00:22:21.839 --> 00:22:25.627
Excellent question once again
from our listening audience.

00:22:25.627 --> 00:22:27.720
- And just for the point of (mumbles)

00:22:27.720 --> 00:22:30.257
we transitioned from ooh, that's cheap,

00:22:30.257 --> 00:22:33.497
it's made of plastic; to oh,
this is made of plastic, fine.

00:22:33.497 --> 00:22:34.567
I don't have any problems.

00:22:34.567 --> 00:22:35.517
It'll last forever.

00:22:36.537 --> 00:22:38.477
That happened, we're about
the same age probably.

00:22:38.477 --> 00:22:39.407
That happened in our lifetime.

00:22:39.407 --> 00:22:40.240
- It did, it did.

00:22:40.240 --> 00:22:42.807
- Yes, okay, just--
- I totally agree.

00:22:42.807 --> 00:22:45.347
- I don't wanna smack you down too hard.

00:22:45.347 --> 00:22:47.597
Just the Octagon will decide this.

00:22:47.597 --> 00:22:48.430
(laughter)

00:22:48.430 --> 00:22:50.067
Okay, does anyone know the Octagon?

00:22:50.067 --> 00:22:50.900
- I'm sure they do.

00:22:50.900 --> 00:22:53.571
And there's a lot of UFC
fans listening right now.

00:22:53.571 --> 00:22:54.504
(laughter)

00:22:54.504 --> 00:22:56.754
- I don't know what the
Venn diagram is of...

00:22:58.227 --> 00:23:00.537
- Right now, there's a guy calculating

00:23:00.537 --> 00:23:02.647
an equation of kick ass.

00:23:02.647 --> 00:23:05.590
All right now, this is Mike Pumaking.

00:23:06.516 --> 00:23:08.083
- Pumaking?
- Pumaking.

00:23:08.083 --> 00:23:09.327
- Pump making?
- Pump making.

00:23:09.327 --> 00:23:10.645
I don't know.

00:23:10.645 --> 00:23:13.567
These people, I think they're
just screwing with me.

00:23:13.567 --> 00:23:14.777
- You think so?
(laughter)

00:23:14.777 --> 00:23:15.947
- I really do.

00:23:15.947 --> 00:23:17.147
They're like, is it Chuck?

00:23:17.147 --> 00:23:19.307
We're gonna just make up names.

00:23:19.307 --> 00:23:20.597
All right, here we go.

00:23:20.597 --> 00:23:23.227
Mr. Savage, what is something
that you would think

00:23:23.227 --> 00:23:26.497
would be possible to build in the future

00:23:26.497 --> 00:23:30.417
that is the now considered
science fiction?

00:23:30.417 --> 00:23:31.542
- [Neil] Ooh, very good.

00:23:31.542 --> 00:23:32.487
- That's a great question.

00:23:32.487 --> 00:23:35.107
- Room temperature superconduction.

00:23:35.107 --> 00:23:36.357
- Wow!

00:23:36.357 --> 00:23:37.527
Well, you went for a big one!

00:23:37.527 --> 00:23:38.417
- I did!

00:23:38.417 --> 00:23:39.677
I'm really excited about,

00:23:39.677 --> 00:23:40.727
speaking of material science,

00:23:40.727 --> 00:23:41.670
I'm really excited about graphene and--

00:23:41.670 --> 00:23:46.137
- See, he came back to
me, material science.

00:23:46.137 --> 00:23:48.037
- All right, so that's your answer?

00:23:48.037 --> 00:23:49.977
- That's my answer, room
temperature superconduction

00:23:49.977 --> 00:23:53.020
is gonna be, I believe it
will happen in our lifetime.

00:23:54.667 --> 00:23:56.687
- And once that happens,
will we actually be able

00:23:56.687 --> 00:23:59.687
to have space ships that stop and hover?

00:23:59.687 --> 00:24:00.632
- I don't know.

00:24:00.632 --> 00:24:03.157
Actually, one of the things we'll learn

00:24:03.157 --> 00:24:06.467
a significant amount about is our brains,

00:24:06.467 --> 00:24:08.487
the processing power of our brains.

00:24:08.487 --> 00:24:11.177
I feel like as computers
become more powerful,

00:24:11.177 --> 00:24:13.367
they're going to become
a real interesting window

00:24:13.367 --> 00:24:15.597
into consciousness and sentience

00:24:15.597 --> 00:24:18.917
and what it is to think about the thinker.

00:24:18.917 --> 00:24:19.750
- Nice.

00:24:19.750 --> 00:24:21.627
- You know, I hadn't quite
heard it put that way,

00:24:21.627 --> 00:24:23.755
but now that I have, I agree 100%

00:24:23.755 --> 00:24:28.755
because it's hard for the
brain to study itself.

00:24:29.297 --> 00:24:30.967
But if we make computers

00:24:30.967 --> 00:24:34.387
that are becoming better,
better approximations

00:24:34.387 --> 00:24:37.230
of our brain, now we have
something we can study.

00:24:39.027 --> 00:24:41.947
And so we would asymptotically
come to an understanding

00:24:41.947 --> 00:24:44.847
of our brain simply by
making our computers

00:24:44.847 --> 00:24:46.517
that much more complex,

00:24:46.517 --> 00:24:49.377
possibly one day achieving
consciousness themselves

00:24:49.377 --> 00:24:51.129
and becoming our overlords.

00:24:51.129 --> 00:24:52.529
- There you go.

00:24:52.529 --> 00:24:56.397
- I think if we ever
made a machine conscious,

00:24:56.397 --> 00:24:58.284
the first thing it's gonna say to us is

00:24:58.284 --> 00:25:00.044
"What the fuck did you do?"

00:25:02.194 --> 00:25:03.027
- That's so true.

00:25:04.078 --> 00:25:04.911
- All right, next question.

00:25:04.911 --> 00:25:05.744
- All right, cool.

00:25:05.744 --> 00:25:06.577
Good, good, good question.

00:25:06.577 --> 00:25:08.540
- Cosmic Queries version of StarTalk.

00:25:10.200 --> 00:25:12.567
- Now here's somebody,

00:25:12.567 --> 00:25:15.047
he calls himself or herself

00:25:15.907 --> 00:25:18.487
The Dragon Horde of Dice.

00:25:18.487 --> 00:25:19.779
- Dragon Horde of Dice.

00:25:19.779 --> 00:25:20.757
- Okay, there you go.

00:25:20.757 --> 00:25:22.347
- That person's mama probably did not give

00:25:22.347 --> 00:25:23.187
that person that name.

00:25:23.187 --> 00:25:26.117
- No, but I think her name is Kate

00:25:26.117 --> 00:25:28.381
because it says @KateNater.

00:25:28.381 --> 00:25:30.185
- [Neil] Okay.
(laughter)

00:25:30.185 --> 00:25:31.044
- So there you go.

00:25:31.044 --> 00:25:32.224
- "Hey, Adam.

00:25:32.224 --> 00:25:35.794
"Since I know you do cosplay,

00:25:35.794 --> 00:25:39.094
"what is the most complex
mechanical prop costume

00:25:39.094 --> 00:25:40.944
"you've ever tried to build?

00:25:40.944 --> 00:25:43.594
"Like a prop or costume
that had no moving parts

00:25:43.594 --> 00:25:45.290
"or could collapse/expand,

00:25:46.139 --> 00:25:49.047
"or something that you may
have tried to just recreate."

00:25:49.047 --> 00:25:51.027
- Did you make Transformers proud ever?

00:25:51.027 --> 00:25:52.417
- Oh gosh, no!

00:25:52.417 --> 00:25:54.947
I have not done a Transformers costume.

00:25:54.947 --> 00:25:59.457
A few years ago, I've been
obsessed with armor since 1981

00:25:59.457 --> 00:26:01.397
when I went and saw Excalibur with my dad.

00:26:01.397 --> 00:26:02.787
- I love Excalibur.
- John Boorman's film--

00:26:02.787 --> 00:26:04.287
- My top five favorite films.

00:26:04.287 --> 00:26:05.207
- Amazing film.

00:26:05.207 --> 00:26:07.619
- And then I learned disturbingly--

00:26:07.619 --> 00:26:08.987
- Yeah, I know the fact
you're about to tell me.

00:26:08.987 --> 00:26:10.147
Go ahead.

00:26:10.147 --> 00:26:10.980
- Oh really?

00:26:10.980 --> 00:26:11.813
No, no, no maybe not.

00:26:11.813 --> 00:26:13.247
- I don't know the fact, please.

00:26:13.247 --> 00:26:14.567
- Excuse me, I'm having
a private conversation

00:26:14.567 --> 00:26:16.067
with my man here?
(laughter)

00:26:16.067 --> 00:26:20.277
Okay, so Excalibur from the
early 1980s, John Boorman,

00:26:20.277 --> 00:26:22.967
an early film that had
Patrick Stewart in it.

00:26:22.967 --> 00:26:24.637
- Patrick Stewart, Liam Neeson.

00:26:24.637 --> 00:26:25.577
- [Neil] Liam Neeson was in it.

00:26:25.577 --> 00:26:29.117
- Gabriel Byrne.
- And so is Helen Mirren?

00:26:29.117 --> 00:26:31.007
- Oh, so beautiful in that movie.

00:26:31.007 --> 00:26:33.092
- It's the King Arthur story told.

00:26:33.092 --> 00:26:34.147
- Thank you.

00:26:34.147 --> 00:26:37.377
- Much later I realized,

00:26:37.377 --> 00:26:39.067
I think half the reason
why I liked the movie

00:26:39.067 --> 00:26:40.620
was because of the soundtrack.

00:26:41.687 --> 00:26:42.520
- Oh, okay.

00:26:42.520 --> 00:26:45.140
- A very powerful Wagnerian,

00:26:46.607 --> 00:26:48.117
and that which is not Wagnerian

00:26:48.117 --> 00:26:50.107
comes from Carmina Burana.

00:26:50.107 --> 00:26:53.487
Very emotional energetic music

00:26:53.487 --> 00:26:55.637
that you just feel in every scene.

00:26:55.637 --> 00:26:56.517
And I thought to myself,

00:26:56.517 --> 00:26:58.087
maybe the movie's not so good

00:26:58.087 --> 00:26:59.157
but the music was amazing

00:26:59.157 --> 00:27:00.537
and I completely compensated for it.

00:27:00.537 --> 00:27:01.677
- I watched it recently.

00:27:01.677 --> 00:27:05.297
It's still super campy and
still really impressively great.

00:27:05.297 --> 00:27:07.147
- But did you watch it on mute?

00:27:07.147 --> 00:27:08.897
- No.
(laughter)

00:27:08.897 --> 00:27:10.817
- With subtitles and mute.

00:27:10.817 --> 00:27:11.886
Try that next time.

00:27:11.886 --> 00:27:13.057
- I did not apply a control, Chuck.

00:27:13.057 --> 00:27:15.114
- Okay, so your answer.

00:27:15.114 --> 00:27:17.137
- Oh, so I've been obsessed
with armor since then

00:27:17.137 --> 00:27:20.547
and I actually, when I was a
junior in high school in 1984,

00:27:20.547 --> 00:27:23.727
I built a suit of armor
out of roofing aluminum

00:27:23.727 --> 00:27:25.937
with my dad and wore it
to school on Halloween

00:27:25.937 --> 00:27:29.697
and passed out of heat
exhaustion in the third period.

00:27:29.697 --> 00:27:32.997
It's all just up north at
Sleepy Hollow High School.

00:27:32.997 --> 00:27:35.645
- Hence your Twitter handle, donttrythis.

00:27:35.645 --> 00:27:38.207
- Exactly.
- At home.

00:27:38.207 --> 00:27:40.517
- A few years ago, I
called up Terry English

00:27:40.517 --> 00:27:42.127
who's the master armorer that built

00:27:42.127 --> 00:27:43.467
all the armor for Excalibur.

00:27:43.467 --> 00:27:46.177
He lives in the southern
tip of England in Cornwall.

00:27:46.177 --> 00:27:49.357
And I went to his studio
a couple summers ago

00:27:49.357 --> 00:27:52.247
and spent 10 days embedded
with him as his assistant

00:27:52.247 --> 00:27:55.367
while he and I manufactured me a suit

00:27:55.367 --> 00:27:57.087
of King Arthur's armor from Excalibur.

00:27:57.087 --> 00:27:58.097
- [Neil] You were an apprentice!

00:27:58.097 --> 00:28:00.347
- I was an apprentice, and he made me

00:28:00.347 --> 00:28:02.527
my lifetime goal suit of armor

00:28:02.527 --> 00:28:04.167
of Arthur's armor from Excalibur.

00:28:04.167 --> 00:28:05.737
- Wow, pretty intense.

00:28:05.737 --> 00:28:07.007
- It was so intense.

00:28:07.007 --> 00:28:07.957
It was amazing.

00:28:07.957 --> 00:28:09.307
He lives in this incredible

00:28:09.307 --> 00:28:12.694
sort of overgrown shop in Cornwall.

00:28:12.694 --> 00:28:14.517
- So he's you in England.

00:28:14.517 --> 00:28:17.008
- Yeah, totally.
(laughter)

00:28:17.008 --> 00:28:19.052
- Woah, there's only room
for one per continent,

00:28:19.052 --> 00:28:20.885
I think, I don't know.

00:28:21.970 --> 00:28:24.807
- That was by far my favorite
mechanical prop construction.

00:28:24.807 --> 00:28:27.149
- Okay, but it was not
electronic or anything,

00:28:27.149 --> 00:28:28.151
it was just mechanical.

00:28:28.151 --> 00:28:29.639
- No, no, it's hammering
aluminum all day long.

00:28:29.639 --> 00:28:30.472
- Did you have chainmail and everything?

00:28:30.472 --> 00:28:31.959
- Yeah, yeah, absolutely.

00:28:31.959 --> 00:28:34.365
- Wow, that's a pretty wild, man.

00:28:34.365 --> 00:28:35.530
Congratulations on that.

00:28:35.530 --> 00:28:37.251
- You still have it?

00:28:37.251 --> 00:28:38.987
- Oh yeah, yeah, I wear
it every chance I get.

00:28:38.987 --> 00:28:40.624
- How'd you get it through TSA?

00:28:40.624 --> 00:28:43.527
- Actually, when I--

00:28:43.527 --> 00:28:44.637
- Whatever they were known back then.

00:28:44.637 --> 00:28:48.047
- No, I actually took it
to New Zealand last year

00:28:48.047 --> 00:28:50.097
to make a little film with
Peter Jackson about it

00:28:50.097 --> 00:28:51.567
where a demon tears my arm off.

00:28:51.567 --> 00:28:53.570
We called it, "A Farewell to Arms."

00:28:53.570 --> 00:28:55.218
(laughter)

00:28:55.218 --> 00:28:57.487
- Things are getting
weird real quickly here.

00:28:57.487 --> 00:28:59.247
- When I carried the armor with me,

00:28:59.247 --> 00:29:02.317
I was thinking this is priceless to me.

00:29:02.317 --> 00:29:04.187
I can't really insure this.

00:29:04.187 --> 00:29:08.767
So I checked it as luggage
with a GPS transceiver

00:29:08.767 --> 00:29:11.047
in my luggage so I could monitor it

00:29:11.047 --> 00:29:12.717
being loaded on to my plane.

00:29:12.717 --> 00:29:13.550
- That's pretty wild.

00:29:13.550 --> 00:29:14.867
- I felt if the airline was gonna tell me

00:29:14.867 --> 00:29:16.337
they couldn't find it, I wanted to know

00:29:16.337 --> 00:29:17.217
that I could find it.

00:29:17.217 --> 00:29:18.050
- I got to tell you something.

00:29:18.050 --> 00:29:20.377
- Plane goes down, they
can't find the black box

00:29:20.377 --> 00:29:21.430
but he found the--

00:29:21.430 --> 00:29:23.672
- He found the suit of armor.
- We found the suit of armor.

00:29:23.672 --> 00:29:24.710
- I don't know where anybody else is--

00:29:24.710 --> 00:29:27.297
- We have no idea why all
these people are dead,

00:29:27.297 --> 00:29:31.408
but we do have an awesome
suit of armor right now.

00:29:31.408 --> 00:29:34.054
(laughter)

00:29:34.054 --> 00:29:35.427
- Oh my God.

00:29:35.427 --> 00:29:37.797
Wow man, that's pretty wild.

00:29:37.797 --> 00:29:39.614
- You're far weirder than I ever--

00:29:39.614 --> 00:29:42.197
- But wait a minute--
- I extrapolated for you.

00:29:42.197 --> 00:29:43.667
- Wait, you and Peter Jackson

00:29:43.667 --> 00:29:45.100
are making home movies?

00:29:46.015 --> 00:29:47.814
- Occasionally, yeah.
- What's going on?

00:29:47.814 --> 00:29:49.577
- And for those who didn't know,

00:29:49.577 --> 00:29:51.567
the Lord of the Ring series

00:29:51.567 --> 00:29:53.197
was mostly filmed in New Zealand.

00:29:53.197 --> 00:29:54.030
- By Peter Jackson,

00:29:54.030 --> 00:29:55.857
and Peter Jackson was inspired to,

00:29:55.857 --> 00:29:57.237
one of the key inspirations for him

00:29:57.237 --> 00:30:00.037
in making Lord of The Rings was Excalibur.

00:30:00.037 --> 00:30:02.337
- So I'm not the only
one who likes Excalibur?

00:30:02.337 --> 00:30:03.457
It's my top five movies.

00:30:03.457 --> 00:30:04.477
- No, dude, it's a super important film.

00:30:04.477 --> 00:30:06.687
- But other four, really weird not that,

00:30:06.687 --> 00:30:08.397
but that's in there.

00:30:08.397 --> 00:30:10.917
- That's sweet.

00:30:10.917 --> 00:30:12.976
I can't say it's one of my favorite.

00:30:12.976 --> 00:30:15.007
I'm not cool enough.

00:30:15.007 --> 00:30:18.747
- I need help.
- One other thing.

00:30:18.747 --> 00:30:19.580
- Go ahead.

00:30:19.580 --> 00:30:21.217
These five movies are
like the only five movies

00:30:21.217 --> 00:30:25.267
I've ever not only seen
a zillion times but

00:30:25.267 --> 00:30:28.977
sat through the director's narration.

00:30:28.977 --> 00:30:30.357
- [Adam] Oh, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:30:30.357 --> 00:30:31.214
Director's commentary?

00:30:31.214 --> 00:30:32.487
- The director's commentary.

00:30:32.487 --> 00:30:34.437
Because you have to like the movie so much

00:30:34.437 --> 00:30:36.057
that you don't even need to watch

00:30:36.057 --> 00:30:38.397
this next iteration of it

00:30:38.397 --> 00:30:39.917
and hear somebody just talk through it.

00:30:39.917 --> 00:30:40.807
- Nice.

00:30:40.807 --> 00:30:42.617
Well, you've also seen it that many times.

00:30:42.617 --> 00:30:45.047
- Well, that's how you
earn the director's cut

00:30:45.047 --> 00:30:46.067
at that point.
- Right, exactly.

00:30:46.067 --> 00:30:47.777
All right, now I got to know

00:30:47.777 --> 00:30:49.178
what are the other four movies.

00:30:49.178 --> 00:30:50.057
- Yeah, no, I do too.
- Really?

00:30:50.057 --> 00:30:52.497
- Yeah, because if that's
part of the top five,

00:30:52.497 --> 00:30:53.604
and that means there's four other movies

00:30:53.604 --> 00:30:55.607
that made it to director's cut--

00:30:55.607 --> 00:30:56.847
- Number one, "The Matrix."

00:30:56.847 --> 00:30:58.434
- Okay, I'll give you that, "Matrix."

00:30:58.434 --> 00:31:00.394
- Number two, "All That Jazz."

00:31:01.397 --> 00:31:02.818
- With Bob Fosse?
- Bob Fosse.

00:31:02.818 --> 00:31:04.081
- Okay, that's a good one.

00:31:04.081 --> 00:31:06.237
- Roy Scheider.
- We have Roy Scheider

00:31:06.237 --> 00:31:08.487
sort of playing Bob Fosse, okay?

00:31:08.487 --> 00:31:10.677
Three, "The Conversation."

00:31:10.677 --> 00:31:12.870
- Oh, such a good movie!

00:31:12.870 --> 00:31:14.276
- Oh man, now I'm lost.

00:31:14.276 --> 00:31:15.109
- Francis Ford Coppola.

00:31:15.109 --> 00:31:16.481
- Coppola's "The Conversation."

00:31:16.481 --> 00:31:18.297
- "The Conversation."

00:31:18.297 --> 00:31:19.574
Four, it's a toss up between--

00:31:19.574 --> 00:31:21.227
- Oh wait, "The Conversation,"

00:31:21.227 --> 00:31:22.377
that's with the man and the woman?

00:31:22.377 --> 00:31:23.462
- Yes.
- Okay, go ahead.

00:31:23.462 --> 00:31:24.524
(laughter)

00:31:24.524 --> 00:31:26.435
- That movie with the man and woman.

00:31:26.435 --> 00:31:28.181
- Okay, all right, go
ahead, go ahead, go ahead,

00:31:28.181 --> 00:31:29.707
- "Conversation."

00:31:29.707 --> 00:31:32.577
And then there's probably a toss up here

00:31:32.577 --> 00:31:37.577
but the "West Side Story."

00:31:37.577 --> 00:31:40.021
- [Chuck] Amazing, that's great.

00:31:40.021 --> 00:31:42.277
- And then "Excalibur."

00:31:42.277 --> 00:31:44.487
I think it's fifth out of those five.

00:31:44.487 --> 00:31:46.557
- Look, I say there's at least
nine films in my top five.

00:31:46.557 --> 00:31:49.177
- There are nine films in my top five.

00:31:49.177 --> 00:31:51.597
- Top five lists actually can go quantum

00:31:51.597 --> 00:31:52.430
and we could fit lots of--

00:31:52.430 --> 00:31:53.827
- We get another dimension--

00:31:53.827 --> 00:31:55.896
- You've got timelines on your--

00:31:55.896 --> 00:31:58.927
- So those five are so unlike each other--

00:31:58.927 --> 00:32:00.457
- And what an eclectic mix--

00:32:00.457 --> 00:32:02.267
- It's really weird but I totally,

00:32:02.267 --> 00:32:04.357
so I used to dance, okay?

00:32:04.357 --> 00:32:05.807
So hence, the Bob Fosse.

00:32:05.807 --> 00:32:07.777
- Have you done "Dancing With The Stars"?

00:32:07.777 --> 00:32:10.840
- No that's so cliche, dude!

00:32:10.840 --> 00:32:11.757
(overlapping dialogue)

00:32:11.757 --> 00:32:13.587
- I suddenly want to see you on that show.

00:32:13.587 --> 00:32:15.741
- When I was dancing, no
one was publishing my books.

00:32:15.741 --> 00:32:17.327
(mumbles) I don't need to dance anymore.

00:32:17.327 --> 00:32:18.670
Okay.

00:32:18.670 --> 00:32:21.071
So what, Chuck?

00:32:21.071 --> 00:32:21.904
(overlapping dialogue) I'm serious!

00:32:21.904 --> 00:32:22.977
- Do you love "Strictly Ballroom"?

00:32:22.977 --> 00:32:24.800
- I'm dancing for nobody, okay?

00:32:26.007 --> 00:32:27.954
My people have come a long way.

00:32:27.954 --> 00:32:31.287
(laughter)
- Copy that.

00:32:31.287 --> 00:32:33.051
- Okay.
- Okay?

00:32:33.051 --> 00:32:37.347
So there's that, and I wrote a column

00:32:37.347 --> 00:32:40.037
for a magazine under
the pen name of Merlin,

00:32:40.037 --> 00:32:42.377
so I have extra interest

00:32:42.377 --> 00:32:45.077
in the Merlin Arthurian legends

00:32:45.077 --> 00:32:47.777
and I have the Malory book in velum,

00:32:47.777 --> 00:32:49.384
you know, so there's that.

00:32:49.384 --> 00:32:51.747
"Matrix" of course is the,
"Matrix" one of course.

00:32:51.747 --> 00:32:52.647
Not two and three.
- Absolutely perfect.

00:32:52.647 --> 00:32:53.480
- It has to be there?

00:32:53.480 --> 00:32:54.827
- There are two other movies?

00:32:54.827 --> 00:32:59.827
- And "The Conversation"
which is so, so well done.

00:32:59.837 --> 00:33:01.777
- An amazing film.

00:33:01.777 --> 00:33:04.607
- There's no explosions,
there's no chase scenes.

00:33:04.607 --> 00:33:07.647
It was a story well done.

00:33:07.647 --> 00:33:10.177
And so there I am.
- Nice!

00:33:10.177 --> 00:33:11.010
- Those are my five.

00:33:11.010 --> 00:33:13.067
- Now listen, so there you go people,

00:33:13.067 --> 00:33:14.517
now you know what you're doing,

00:33:14.517 --> 00:33:16.620
Netflix and chill with Neil's list.

00:33:16.620 --> 00:33:19.207
(laughter)

00:33:19.207 --> 00:33:20.317
- That's a weird list.

00:33:20.317 --> 00:33:22.614
- What a weird list to
Netflix and chill, right?

00:33:22.614 --> 00:33:25.427
- Oh, and "West Side Story" is New York.

00:33:25.427 --> 00:33:28.400
It's the city, it's I'm half Puerto Rican.

00:33:29.427 --> 00:33:32.057
My mother's name is Sunchita
Maria Feliciano Tyson.

00:33:32.057 --> 00:33:32.890
Okay, that's her name.

00:33:32.890 --> 00:33:34.687
- "West Side Story" is such a masterpiece.

00:33:34.687 --> 00:33:36.234
- It's a masterpiece.
- A masterpiece.

00:33:36.234 --> 00:33:38.237
- Masterpiece! Masterpiece!

00:33:38.237 --> 00:33:41.907
And while I don't know
how to compose music,

00:33:41.907 --> 00:33:46.357
if I ever could, I'm
imagining that I could compose

00:33:46.357 --> 00:33:49.377
the music that we hear
in "West Side Story".

00:33:49.377 --> 00:33:50.327
That's where I'm thinking,

00:33:50.327 --> 00:33:51.827
that's what I would reach for.

00:33:52.917 --> 00:33:54.367
- That would be your North Star.

00:33:54.367 --> 00:33:55.525
- That'd be my North Star.

00:33:55.525 --> 00:33:58.197
He's beautiful, I love Adam.

