And this is, you know, it's
a window into your brain.

00:35:16.687 --> 00:35:18.120
- Oh yeah, there's some
organized shelves--

00:35:18.120 --> 00:35:20.377
- I like the fact that you
have photos in it, thanks.

00:35:20.377 --> 00:35:22.287
- Oh yeah.
- Thank your publisher

00:35:22.287 --> 00:35:23.957
for agreeing to, because
they don't always agree

00:35:23.957 --> 00:35:25.306
to put pictures in their books.

00:35:25.306 --> 00:35:26.990
- No they did, I threw
tons into the manuscript

00:35:26.990 --> 00:35:29.667
and after we had the manuscript approved,

00:35:29.667 --> 00:35:31.244
they said, "Okay, so you're responsible

00:35:31.244 --> 00:35:33.441
"for all the permissions for the photos?"

00:35:33.441 --> 00:35:35.123
- Oh.
(laughter)

00:35:35.123 --> 00:35:36.267
- Publishers are such wimps
when it comes to that.

00:35:36.267 --> 00:35:38.857
Oh man, don't get me started.

00:35:38.857 --> 00:35:40.397
Yeah, so definitely.

00:35:40.397 --> 00:35:41.867
So next question, what was that?

00:35:41.867 --> 00:35:45.257
- All right, so this
was Lane from Minnesota

00:35:45.257 --> 00:35:48.254
who said, "As a builder,
I remember failed projects

00:35:48.254 --> 00:35:50.087
"more then the successes."

00:35:50.087 --> 00:35:50.920
- Stop there.

00:35:50.920 --> 00:35:53.637
So tell me about failures
relative to successes.

00:35:53.637 --> 00:35:55.897
- Well, we talk about
this a lot these days.

00:35:55.897 --> 00:35:57.657
Failure is a really popular word.

00:35:57.657 --> 00:35:59.744
And Silicon Valley says, you know,

00:35:59.744 --> 00:36:01.677
"Build fast and break things."

00:36:01.677 --> 00:36:02.867
We talk about educating kids,

00:36:02.867 --> 00:36:04.807
we talk about teaching them how to fail.

00:36:04.807 --> 00:36:06.630
And I have a particular take on this

00:36:06.630 --> 00:36:08.857
because I think failure is a fine word--

00:36:08.857 --> 00:36:12.188
- But there's a version of
that from the '90s or '80s,

00:36:12.188 --> 00:36:14.187
which was shocking when we first heard it,

00:36:14.187 --> 00:36:17.877
was, "If it ain't broke, break it."

00:36:17.877 --> 00:36:20.227
- Break it.
- Yes.

00:36:20.227 --> 00:36:22.577
- So that way, you might
invent something better

00:36:22.577 --> 00:36:23.897
than the thing you had before.

00:36:23.897 --> 00:36:24.730
And just because it worked,

00:36:24.730 --> 00:36:26.177
doesn't mean that's the best
version of what it could be.

00:36:26.177 --> 00:36:27.552
- Indeed.

00:36:27.552 --> 00:36:28.427
- Or you just end up with
a lot of broken shit.

00:36:28.427 --> 00:36:29.317
(laughter)

00:36:29.317 --> 00:36:30.770
- That's if you're creative.

00:36:31.657 --> 00:36:33.267
- There's always broken shit behind me.

00:36:33.267 --> 00:36:35.377
(overlapping dialogue)
- Exactly.

00:36:35.377 --> 00:36:36.210
- All right.
- Go ahead.

00:36:36.210 --> 00:36:39.037
- But we don't really mean failure.

00:36:39.037 --> 00:36:42.507
Wrong turns on the path
to get to a success

00:36:42.507 --> 00:36:45.437
are not failures, they're
simply iterations.

00:36:45.437 --> 00:36:47.417
And I'm here--

00:36:47.417 --> 00:36:48.757
- And that's not a euphemism or anything?

00:36:48.757 --> 00:36:50.887
- No my--
- Smells like one.

00:36:50.887 --> 00:36:53.657
- What I wanna preach is
that both science and art

00:36:53.657 --> 00:36:56.667
are iterative processes that
include tons of wrong turns.

00:36:56.667 --> 00:36:58.047
And the wrong turns aren't outliers,

00:36:58.047 --> 00:37:00.267
they're part of that process.

00:37:00.267 --> 00:37:01.617
And we go up the path and we're like,

00:37:01.617 --> 00:37:02.607
okay, this isn't the path

00:37:02.607 --> 00:37:03.797
so let's go back to the fork

00:37:03.797 --> 00:37:05.527
and continue down another path

00:37:05.527 --> 00:37:06.927
and see if that's the path.

00:37:06.927 --> 00:37:08.997
And so yes, I also remember my failures

00:37:08.997 --> 00:37:11.957
more than my successes because
that's where I learned.

00:37:11.957 --> 00:37:13.987
The failures, the failures.

00:37:13.987 --> 00:37:17.117
The wrong turns I took
to get to the right turns

00:37:17.117 --> 00:37:20.247
are the places that I
learned the most about myself

00:37:20.247 --> 00:37:21.447
and about how to build things

00:37:21.447 --> 00:37:23.437
and about how to recover
from those moments.

00:37:23.437 --> 00:37:25.807
- You know what they say
about rocket launch failures?

00:37:25.807 --> 00:37:26.972
That they're not failures,

00:37:26.972 --> 00:37:29.052
they're just experiments rich in data.

00:37:29.052 --> 00:37:30.820
(laughter)

00:37:30.820 --> 00:37:32.147
- Right in the movies,

00:37:32.147 --> 00:37:33.407
there's always the guy who says

00:37:33.407 --> 00:37:35.187
my experiment was a failure.

00:37:35.187 --> 00:37:36.357
No scientist says that.

00:37:36.357 --> 00:37:37.577
- No we don't actually.

00:37:37.577 --> 00:37:40.527
If it's well designed, a negative result

00:37:40.527 --> 00:37:41.937
is actually useful information to you.

00:37:41.937 --> 00:37:43.648
- Indeed.
- Publishable information.

00:37:43.648 --> 00:37:45.637
- You have to say that

00:37:45.637 --> 00:37:47.939
when somebody else is
paying for what you did.

00:37:47.939 --> 00:37:51.067
- Yeah, I was just reading
an article the other day

00:37:51.067 --> 00:37:53.537
about how journals, publishing journals,

00:37:53.537 --> 00:37:56.567
don't highlight null results.

00:37:56.567 --> 00:37:57.694
- They don't value it in the publishing--

00:37:57.694 --> 00:37:59.313
- [Adam] They don't value
it in the publishing--

00:37:59.313 --> 00:38:00.597
- Yeah, yeah, and we should more.

00:38:00.597 --> 00:38:01.430
It's a problem.

00:38:01.430 --> 00:38:03.467
- That's true, I never thought of that.

00:38:03.467 --> 00:38:06.647
But yeah, there is a great deal of value--

00:38:06.647 --> 00:38:07.857
- Tells you what not to do.
(overlapping dialogue)

00:38:07.857 --> 00:38:09.857
- Exactly, it tells the next person

00:38:09.857 --> 00:38:12.377
look, this is what I
did, it didn't work out

00:38:12.377 --> 00:38:15.457
and now I don't have to
waste time going down that--

00:38:15.457 --> 00:38:17.077
- That's a scientific inquiry

00:38:17.077 --> 00:38:18.457
that is equally as important

00:38:18.457 --> 00:38:19.947
as some brand new fact about--

00:38:19.947 --> 00:38:21.666
- Oh no, that makes a lot of sense.

00:38:21.666 --> 00:38:24.277
- You know, Chuck has a new
hero of the recent months

00:38:24.277 --> 00:38:26.317
who may have been
responsible for sending us

00:38:26.317 --> 00:38:31.317
down the wrong path,
because his mantra was

00:38:31.917 --> 00:38:34.257
failure is not an option.

00:38:34.257 --> 00:38:35.797
This was from Apollo 13.

00:38:35.797 --> 00:38:38.507
And this is Gene Kranz.

00:38:38.507 --> 00:38:40.147
So failure is not an option.

00:38:40.147 --> 00:38:41.467
And then that became America.

00:38:41.467 --> 00:38:42.727
We're not gonna fail.

00:38:42.727 --> 00:38:45.077
And then we lost track
of the value of failure.

00:38:46.967 --> 00:38:49.187
- The most surprising
thing I learned from doing

00:38:49.187 --> 00:38:51.157
MythBusters for 13 years

00:38:51.157 --> 00:38:54.947
was how surprisingly creative
the scientific discipline is

00:38:54.947 --> 00:38:57.307
and how it mirrors the
creative discipline.

00:38:57.307 --> 00:39:00.187
To me, conducting experiments,

00:39:00.187 --> 00:39:02.437
conducting and building
experimental methodologies

00:39:02.437 --> 00:39:05.607
is every bit as creatively satisfying

00:39:05.607 --> 00:39:07.147
as making a sculpture, painting a painting

00:39:07.147 --> 00:39:08.747
or writing a book.
- I agree 100%.

00:39:08.747 --> 00:39:11.977
- But I think it's better
when you call it a wrong turn,

00:39:11.977 --> 00:39:13.677
the way you put it that way.

00:39:13.677 --> 00:39:14.907
Because if you think about it,

00:39:14.907 --> 00:39:19.907
just from a standpoint
everybody can identify--

00:39:19.917 --> 00:39:21.797
- A driver's standpoint.
- Driver's standpoint.

00:39:21.797 --> 00:39:23.007
When you make a wrong turn,

00:39:23.007 --> 00:39:24.427
how many times have you done that

00:39:24.427 --> 00:39:27.677
and discovered something
that you really liked?

00:39:27.677 --> 00:39:29.303
- Oh, there's botanical gardens over here.

00:39:29.303 --> 00:39:30.757
(overlapping dialogue)
- Oh my God, who knew?

00:39:30.757 --> 00:39:35.077
- That process is the soul
of standup comedy, right?

00:39:35.077 --> 00:39:37.254
Iterate, iterate, iterate
every joke, every joke.

00:39:37.254 --> 00:39:40.019
- Listen, it's the same process

00:39:40.019 --> 00:39:42.147
even when the joke works.
- Yes!

00:39:42.147 --> 00:39:44.837
You go back and you try to crack it open

00:39:44.837 --> 00:39:46.246
and see how can I make this even better?

00:39:46.246 --> 00:39:47.079
(overlapping dialogue)
- If it works, break it.

00:39:47.079 --> 00:39:48.134
- Indeed.
- Yeah, no, &gt;

00:39:48.134 --> 00:39:49.894
Yeah, all right, super cool.

00:39:49.894 --> 00:39:50.727
- Next question.
- Wow, Lane.

00:39:50.727 --> 00:39:51.560
What a great question.

00:39:51.560 --> 00:39:53.037
You're a hero, Lane.

00:39:53.037 --> 00:39:54.115
That's what you are.

00:39:54.115 --> 00:39:54.948
You are.

00:39:54.948 --> 00:39:56.837
All right, this is Chris Goes to the Park.

00:39:56.837 --> 00:39:58.172
Thank you Chris.

00:39:58.172 --> 00:40:00.374
(laughter)

00:40:00.374 --> 00:40:02.577
Thanks Chris.

00:40:02.577 --> 00:40:05.964
He said, "Hey, Adam,
are there any inventions

00:40:05.964 --> 00:40:08.684
"your team created on MythBusters

00:40:08.684 --> 00:40:13.394
"that you have kept or that
you might still use today?

00:40:13.394 --> 00:40:14.717
"P.S. loved your show growing up.

00:40:14.717 --> 00:40:16.951
It inspired me to study
engineering, now I'm an engineer.

00:40:16.951 --> 00:40:20.657
- Ooh, ooh.
- Always amazing to hear.

00:40:20.657 --> 00:40:23.627
There's nothing that we kept
from the public on MythBusters

00:40:23.627 --> 00:40:25.197
except for one thing.

00:40:25.197 --> 00:40:26.377
- Ooh.

00:40:26.377 --> 00:40:27.627
- Kari, Grant and Tory were--

00:40:27.627 --> 00:40:28.967
- Wait, wait, I didn't
understand the question.

00:40:28.967 --> 00:40:30.340
You mean, is there something
you made on MythBusters

00:40:30.340 --> 00:40:31.936
that the public never saw?

00:40:31.936 --> 00:40:33.250
(overlapping dialogue)

00:40:33.250 --> 00:40:34.827
- Something we came up with

00:40:34.827 --> 00:40:36.727
or that we discovered on MythBusters

00:40:36.727 --> 00:40:38.967
that we have somehow kept from the public.

00:40:38.967 --> 00:40:40.259
- That's not how I read the question.

00:40:40.259 --> 00:40:41.092
- Well, no.

00:40:41.092 --> 00:40:41.925
He meant, but I like that.

00:40:41.925 --> 00:40:44.147
What he meant, is there
anything that you invented

00:40:44.147 --> 00:40:45.327
or came up that you were like wow,

00:40:45.327 --> 00:40:47.307
this is so good I'm actually
gonna put it to use in my life.

00:40:47.307 --> 00:40:49.573
- Right, that's how I read it.

00:40:49.573 --> 00:40:51.697
- Wow.

00:40:51.697 --> 00:40:54.677
Yes, I mean, really it
was the more the process

00:40:54.677 --> 00:40:56.437
of telling the stories on MythBusters.

00:40:56.437 --> 00:40:58.717
- But wait, that's not
as interesting a question

00:40:58.717 --> 00:40:59.918
as the one you thought I was.

00:40:59.918 --> 00:41:01.883
- I know, I was gonna say.
(laughter)

00:41:01.883 --> 00:41:03.612
- Let's assume it was the
question you thought it was.

00:41:03.612 --> 00:41:04.810
- Exactly, which is?

00:41:04.810 --> 00:41:07.107
- Kari, Grant and Tory were
testing the explosive--

00:41:07.107 --> 00:41:07.940
- Who, who?

00:41:07.940 --> 00:41:09.347
Sorry, my co-hosts on MythBusters,

00:41:09.347 --> 00:41:11.720
Keri Byron, Tory Belleci
and Grant Imahara.

00:41:12.737 --> 00:41:15.357
They were testing a story that involved

00:41:15.357 --> 00:41:19.257
a commonly available material

00:41:19.257 --> 00:41:21.517
that is as close to a description

00:41:21.517 --> 00:41:24.467
as I'm going to get, and
its explosive properties.

00:41:24.467 --> 00:41:27.827
And what they determined
with this use case scenario

00:41:27.827 --> 00:41:32.217
was so spectacularly
terrifyingly explosive

00:41:32.217 --> 00:41:35.297
that we agreed to destroy the
footage of the explanation

00:41:35.297 --> 00:41:38.378
and never tell how we got to that.

00:41:38.378 --> 00:41:40.517
- And I thank you for that.
- Oh yeah, no.

00:41:40.517 --> 00:41:41.907
- Bomb squads the world over

00:41:41.907 --> 00:41:45.352
know what we've figured out on our own

00:41:45.352 --> 00:41:47.757
and are thankful that we've decided

00:41:47.757 --> 00:41:49.447
not to put that in the episode.

00:41:49.447 --> 00:41:50.757
- So yeah, it's like--

00:41:50.757 --> 00:41:52.677
- But Engineer Ben just
found it on YouTube.

00:41:52.677 --> 00:41:53.959
- Right.
(laughter)

00:41:53.959 --> 00:41:55.217
Did you know if you took cotton balls

00:41:55.217 --> 00:41:57.447
and chewing gum, you
could actually bring down

00:41:57.447 --> 00:41:58.667
the World Trade Center?

00:41:58.667 --> 00:42:00.057
Like what?

00:42:00.057 --> 00:42:01.037
Yeah, no, that's crazy.

00:42:01.037 --> 00:42:03.369
- So the answer is yes, you did--

00:42:03.369 --> 00:42:04.202
(overlapping dialogue)
- Yes, yeah.

00:42:04.202 --> 00:42:05.975
- So you quarantined that information

00:42:05.975 --> 00:42:07.807
and just said, to hell with it.

00:42:07.807 --> 00:42:08.737
- The world doesn't need it.

00:42:08.737 --> 00:42:11.517
It will not be better for
this bit of knowledge.

00:42:11.517 --> 00:42:12.949
- Now just between us, what was the...

00:42:12.949 --> 00:42:14.151
(laughter)

00:42:14.151 --> 00:42:15.391
What were the materials?

00:42:15.391 --> 00:42:17.079
- Just between you and
us, no one's listening.

00:42:17.079 --> 00:42:18.699
- What were the materials?

00:42:18.699 --> 00:42:20.967
(laughter)

00:42:20.967 --> 00:42:23.397
All right, I don't wanna know to be honest

00:42:23.397 --> 00:42:25.107
because I can't be trusted.

00:42:25.107 --> 00:42:28.724
All right, this is Ringa Millie who says,

00:42:28.724 --> 00:42:30.804
"How do you create new things

00:42:30.804 --> 00:42:33.637
"that haven't before been thought of?"

00:42:33.637 --> 00:42:36.127
In other words, what is your process

00:42:36.127 --> 00:42:38.870
for creative discovery?

00:42:40.197 --> 00:42:42.305
Is there any process that says,

00:42:42.305 --> 00:42:43.177
well, you know what?

00:42:43.177 --> 00:42:44.010
Boom.

00:42:44.010 --> 00:42:46.067
- Well, I don't know if it's reasonable

00:42:46.067 --> 00:42:48.317
to think about something
as, I want to make something

00:42:48.317 --> 00:42:49.497
that's never been thought of.

00:42:49.497 --> 00:42:52.287
I don't think we have the choice

00:42:52.287 --> 00:42:55.257
to sequester an invention or a build

00:42:55.257 --> 00:42:57.367
or something creative into a category

00:42:57.367 --> 00:42:58.937
before it's been made.

00:42:58.937 --> 00:43:01.447
And everything we made is based

00:43:01.447 --> 00:43:02.837
on everything that we've seen.

00:43:02.837 --> 00:43:05.194
So is it Newton that said,

00:43:05.194 --> 00:43:06.807
"We stand on the shoulders of giants?"

00:43:06.807 --> 00:43:08.287
- Yes.
- Just as Newton says that,

00:43:08.287 --> 00:43:09.544
we all do, it's called--

00:43:09.544 --> 00:43:11.124
- "If I see farther than others,

00:43:11.124 --> 00:43:13.244
"it is because I stood on
the shoulders of giants

00:43:13.244 --> 00:43:14.314
"who have come before me."

00:43:14.314 --> 00:43:16.597
And that is one of the greatest
descriptions of culture

00:43:16.597 --> 00:43:18.478
that anyone's ever penned.

00:43:18.478 --> 00:43:20.557
That's exactly what's happening.

00:43:20.557 --> 00:43:21.847
When I was, you know--

00:43:21.847 --> 00:43:23.467
- Especially now.
- When I was 17,

00:43:23.467 --> 00:43:24.457
I saw "Alien".

00:43:24.457 --> 00:43:26.327
It took me five years to get the aesthetic

00:43:26.327 --> 00:43:28.167
of H.R. Giger out of my system.

00:43:28.167 --> 00:43:30.837
I had to recapitulate the aesthetics

00:43:30.837 --> 00:43:32.827
that he was doing because
I found it so powerful,

00:43:32.827 --> 00:43:34.437
until I kind of understood it.

00:43:34.437 --> 00:43:35.517
That recapitulation--

00:43:35.517 --> 00:43:37.565
- So that person is what to the movie?

00:43:37.565 --> 00:43:40.329
- Yeah, H.R. Giger
designed the alien monster.

00:43:40.329 --> 00:43:41.609
- You said it like
well, but, we all have--

00:43:41.609 --> 00:43:42.442
(overlapping dialogue)
- I know, I'm sorry.

00:43:42.442 --> 00:43:44.687
H.R. Giger is this crazy German

00:43:44.687 --> 00:43:48.467
who designed all of the
creatures and alien technology

00:43:48.467 --> 00:43:50.305
from the original movie "Alien",

00:43:50.305 --> 00:43:52.940
Ridley Scott's first science fiction.

00:43:53.857 --> 00:43:55.697
- Of course with Sigourney Weaver.

00:43:55.697 --> 00:43:58.227
- Sigourney Weaver, Tom
Skerritt, Harry Dean Stanton.

00:43:58.227 --> 00:43:59.977
That's one of my top five films.

00:43:59.977 --> 00:44:01.107
An amazing film.

00:44:01.107 --> 00:44:02.177
I mean, it's a gothic horror film,

00:44:02.177 --> 00:44:03.877
not a science fiction movie but...

00:44:05.577 --> 00:44:07.457
- My issue with it was that the alien

00:44:07.457 --> 00:44:12.057
still had like a mouth, a jaw, teeth.

00:44:12.057 --> 00:44:13.517
- Yeah, but he had a mouth in a mouth.

00:44:13.517 --> 00:44:15.389
I mean, that's pretty creative.

00:44:15.389 --> 00:44:17.417
- No, no, I'm just thinking

00:44:17.417 --> 00:44:21.237
most life forms on Earth do
not have a mouth and teeth.

00:44:21.237 --> 00:44:23.977
Like trees and worms and
butterflies and this sort of thing.

00:44:23.977 --> 00:44:27.537
So if you're gonna have a complete alien,

00:44:27.537 --> 00:44:30.487
you should be more
creative than even that.

00:44:30.487 --> 00:44:33.637
Like "The Blob", 1958,
Steve McQueen movie.

00:44:33.637 --> 00:44:35.917
- Yes.
- It's got no bones.

00:44:35.917 --> 00:44:37.167
- Nothing.
- It's a blob.

00:44:37.167 --> 00:44:38.000
- It's terrifying.

00:44:38.000 --> 00:44:38.833
- What was it?

00:44:38.833 --> 00:44:40.017
It was a blob.
- It was a blob.

00:44:40.017 --> 00:44:40.850
- It was completely terrifying

00:44:40.850 --> 00:44:42.907
and it would eat your
blood, suck your blood.

00:44:42.907 --> 00:44:44.117
And you know when the blob first landed,

00:44:44.117 --> 00:44:45.157
you know what color it was?

00:44:45.157 --> 00:44:46.647
It was transparent.

00:44:46.647 --> 00:44:48.707
It only turned red after
it ate its first victim.

00:44:48.707 --> 00:44:49.540
- Woah.
- Yes!

00:44:49.540 --> 00:44:52.001
You didn't know that!
- I did not know that.

00:44:52.001 --> 00:44:53.409
- Oh he didn't know that!

00:44:53.409 --> 00:44:54.889
(overlapping dialogue) I know that.

00:44:54.889 --> 00:44:56.101
I know that!
(laughter)

00:44:56.101 --> 00:44:57.497
And it could come through the
vents, into the (mumbles).

00:44:57.497 --> 00:44:59.917
So to me that's creativity.

00:44:59.917 --> 00:45:01.187
- Yeah, yeah.

00:45:01.187 --> 00:45:04.277
So I don't think that
you can say to yourself

00:45:04.277 --> 00:45:05.977
I wanna think of something
that's never been thought.

00:45:05.977 --> 00:45:08.307
I think you have to just keep on thinking,

00:45:08.307 --> 00:45:11.517
what do I want that doesn't exist?

00:45:11.517 --> 00:45:13.518
What do I want to make extant

00:45:13.518 --> 00:45:17.482
that I can't obtain or I can't get?

00:45:17.482 --> 00:45:20.037
- Oh, so your urges are
guiding your creativity.

00:45:20.037 --> 00:45:22.017
- Totally, absolutely.

00:45:22.017 --> 00:45:24.337
And actually, that's
what this book is to me,

00:45:24.337 --> 00:45:26.307
it's a permission slip to everyone

00:45:26.307 --> 00:45:27.797
to follow those weird urges.

00:45:27.797 --> 00:45:29.097
I call them secret thrills.

00:45:29.097 --> 00:45:31.875
- So you should have titled
it Follow Your Weird Urges.

00:45:31.875 --> 00:45:33.247
(laughter)

00:45:33.247 --> 00:45:34.937
It'd be a bestseller overnight.

00:45:34.937 --> 00:45:35.997
- The publisher rejected that--

00:45:35.997 --> 00:45:37.727
(overlapping dialogue)

00:45:37.727 --> 00:45:39.026
- Is it too late to change it?

00:45:39.026 --> 00:45:39.859
(overlapping dialogue)

00:45:39.859 --> 00:45:41.551
Because that's a damn good title.

00:45:41.551 --> 00:45:44.208
(overlapping dialogue)
Follow Your Weird Urges.

00:45:44.208 --> 00:45:46.510
- I know what I'm signing
in your book, Neil.

00:45:47.558 --> 00:45:50.457
- So we have to go into
lightning round now.

00:45:50.457 --> 00:45:52.427
Okay, we have a few minutes left.

00:45:52.427 --> 00:45:54.767
I need from you sound bite answers.

00:45:54.767 --> 00:45:56.179
Okay, are you ready?
- Yup.

00:45:56.179 --> 00:45:57.139
(bell rings)
- Chuck, go.

00:45:57.139 --> 00:45:57.972
- All right, here we go.

00:45:57.972 --> 00:45:59.747
This is Olivia Waits from Instagram.

00:45:59.747 --> 00:46:01.327
She wants to know, how long does it take

00:46:01.327 --> 00:46:05.080
to usually come up with a truly new idea?

00:46:06.127 --> 00:46:07.892
- That is impossible to quantify.

00:46:07.892 --> 00:46:08.892
(bell rings)
- Next.

00:46:08.892 --> 00:46:09.877
- There you go.

00:46:09.877 --> 00:46:11.437
Cana Slicker says--

00:46:11.437 --> 00:46:13.147
- Wait, let me ask, what's the longest

00:46:13.147 --> 00:46:15.780
and shortest time it took you
to come up with a new idea?

00:46:16.718 --> 00:46:17.816
- Again, impossible to quantify.

00:46:17.816 --> 00:46:18.887
- Get the range.

00:46:18.887 --> 00:46:21.487
- The range, between
one minute and 17 hours.

00:46:21.487 --> 00:46:22.320
- Okay (bell rings) good.

00:46:22.320 --> 00:46:23.517
- There you go, I'll accept it.

00:46:23.517 --> 00:46:25.149
- He pulled that one out
of his ass, the 17 hours.

00:46:25.149 --> 00:46:25.982
(overlapping dialogue)

00:46:25.982 --> 00:46:27.142
- Sorry, we were looking for two days.

00:46:27.142 --> 00:46:28.690
(laughter)
- Wrong answer!

00:46:30.425 --> 00:46:31.258
All right, go.
- Here we go.

00:46:32.884 --> 00:46:34.564
Canasilicker.

00:46:34.564 --> 00:46:37.387
Oh, (mumbles) I think
somebody made, nevermind.

00:46:37.387 --> 00:46:41.157
What is your most unique out
of left field inspiration

00:46:41.157 --> 00:46:44.767
that has ever happened to
you or even surprised you?

00:46:44.767 --> 00:46:45.600
That is weird.

00:46:45.600 --> 00:46:46.527
Where'd you find inspiration,

00:46:46.527 --> 00:46:48.007
a place that you never thought you would?

00:46:48.007 --> 00:46:49.287
- Good.
- Oh wow.

00:46:49.287 --> 00:46:51.287
I'm gonna need time to
think about that one.

00:46:51.287 --> 00:46:53.176
I don't have a sound
bite answer for that one.

00:46:53.176 --> 00:46:54.037
- You have till the end of the show.

00:46:54.037 --> 00:46:55.141
Okay, next question.

00:46:55.141 --> 00:46:55.974
- Okay, here we go.

00:46:55.974 --> 00:46:57.147
Julie--
- But wait,

00:46:57.147 --> 00:46:59.227
let me reword that question.

00:46:59.227 --> 00:47:00.677
Maybe it's the same question.

00:47:01.527 --> 00:47:03.407
Among all your sources of inspiration,

00:47:03.407 --> 00:47:04.957
what has been the most fertile?

00:47:06.125 --> 00:47:10.377
- Oh, actually always reading
about the first experiment

00:47:10.377 --> 00:47:11.607
of something that was discovered.

00:47:11.607 --> 00:47:14.407
Reading Fizeau's speed of light
experiment is so thrilling.

00:47:14.407 --> 00:47:16.017
- Mmm, okay, good, good.

00:47:16.017 --> 00:47:17.417
- Wow, what a great answer

00:47:17.417 --> 00:47:19.554
and an even better question.

00:47:19.554 --> 00:47:21.070
- Fizeau as in Doppler-Fizeau.

00:47:21.070 --> 00:47:24.587
It's end late 19th century, early--

00:47:24.587 --> 00:47:25.887
- He calculated the speed of light

00:47:25.887 --> 00:47:27.927
with clock work and mirrors.

00:47:27.927 --> 00:47:29.177
- Okay, there we go.

00:47:29.177 --> 00:47:31.017
Next. (bell rings)
- There we go.

00:47:31.017 --> 00:47:35.307
This is Juliek from NB says, or fromed,

00:47:35.307 --> 00:47:40.307
says, if you had unlimited
resources and time,

00:47:41.347 --> 00:47:43.157
what would you build?

00:47:43.157 --> 00:47:45.077
- Ooh.
- Oh, I'd build a spaceship

00:47:45.077 --> 00:47:47.253
and I'd go to the moon
and then I'd go to Mars.

00:47:47.253 --> 00:47:49.447
With unlimited resources
and material, yeah, totally.

00:47:49.447 --> 00:47:51.527
I would start some
interplanetary exploration.

00:47:51.527 --> 00:47:52.360
- Beautiful, love it.

00:47:52.360 --> 00:47:55.047
In this office, I love that answer.

00:47:55.047 --> 00:47:56.428
- [Chuck] All right, last question?

00:47:56.428 --> 00:47:57.261
That's it?

00:47:57.261 --> 00:47:58.620
- No, no, I don't think we have time.

00:47:58.620 --> 00:47:59.757
Just final reflection.

00:47:59.757 --> 00:48:02.078
Chuck, give me your final reflection.

00:48:02.078 --> 00:48:05.677
- I am going to now pursue my weird urges.

00:48:05.677 --> 00:48:08.005
- Weird urges.
- And I am really happy--

00:48:08.005 --> 00:48:09.307
(overlapping dialogue)
- Go for it.

00:48:09.307 --> 00:48:10.677
- I'm happy I have permission to do so.

00:48:10.677 --> 00:48:12.800
- [Neil] Okay. Adam?

00:48:14.147 --> 00:48:17.217
You got this book, presumably
there's still stuff in you

00:48:17.217 --> 00:48:18.050
that's not in this book

00:48:18.050 --> 00:48:19.197
otherwise buy the book
and then we don't have

00:48:19.197 --> 00:48:21.297
to ever watch you again, right?

00:48:21.297 --> 00:48:22.287
- Oh yeah, I held some stuff back.

00:48:22.287 --> 00:48:23.200
- Tell me something that's not in the book

00:48:23.200 --> 00:48:24.767
that we should carry with us.

00:48:24.767 --> 00:48:26.630
- Oh wow.

00:48:28.737 --> 00:48:30.867
That all human beings really wanna do

00:48:30.867 --> 00:48:32.647
is tell each other stories.

00:48:32.647 --> 00:48:34.967
And that's both science and art.

00:48:34.967 --> 00:48:36.377
It's how we understand the universe.

00:48:36.377 --> 00:48:37.777
- Even if the story you're telling

00:48:37.777 --> 00:48:38.610
is how to build something.

00:48:38.610 --> 00:48:41.142
- Indeed.
- Ooh, I like it.

00:48:41.142 --> 00:48:42.547
Very nice.

00:48:42.547 --> 00:48:44.377
- So what I'd like to think if I can add

00:48:44.377 --> 00:48:48.927
to an earlier question about creativity,

00:48:48.927 --> 00:48:50.020
to have a thought,

00:48:50.867 --> 00:48:53.397
to see what everyone else has seen

00:48:53.397 --> 00:48:56.577
and think what no one else has thought,

00:48:56.577 --> 00:48:59.870
that's a commonly invoked
definition of genius.

00:49:00.747 --> 00:49:05.147
But maybe it's not genius
that we're honoring there.

00:49:05.147 --> 00:49:08.667
Maybe it's hard work,

00:49:08.667 --> 00:49:12.797
where you have a lot of
dangling thoughts within you

00:49:12.797 --> 00:49:15.017
because you've read a lot of books.

00:49:15.017 --> 00:49:16.157
You've seen a lot of movies,

00:49:16.157 --> 00:49:17.627
you've spoken to a lot of people.

00:49:17.627 --> 00:49:19.167
You've done a lot of tinkering.

00:49:19.167 --> 00:49:21.647
And they're sitting there
waiting in your head,

00:49:21.647 --> 00:49:26.647
and then you walk amid all
of these dangling parts

00:49:27.107 --> 00:49:29.154
and you say, give me two bits of that

00:49:29.154 --> 00:49:30.660
and one part of that, one part of this

00:49:30.660 --> 00:49:32.877
because they're available
to you to draw from

00:49:32.877 --> 00:49:35.177
and out of that comes a brand new idea,

00:49:35.177 --> 00:49:38.547
a brand new object, a brand new concept.

00:49:38.547 --> 00:49:42.737
So I think the people who are cited

00:49:42.737 --> 00:49:45.497
as the great inventors
and geniuses of the world

00:49:45.497 --> 00:49:48.327
are simply those who
have more bits and pieces

00:49:48.327 --> 00:49:50.957
in their lives waiting to be
assembled into something new.

00:49:50.957 --> 00:49:53.250
- Hmmm.
- I totally agree.

00:49:54.367 --> 00:49:55.327
- Very nice.

00:49:55.327 --> 00:49:57.588
- It's beautiful that you agreed.

00:49:57.588 --> 00:50:00.114
Because I pulled that from
the cosmic perspective.

00:50:00.114 --> 00:50:01.757
(chuckles)

